// AAMC Question Bank Flashcards — Chemistry: original recall questions written from the concepts tested in the
// AAMC Chemistry Question Pack. No AAMC passages, questions or explanations are reproduced.
// Format: {s: tag, disc: scoring discipline, aamc: content category, q, choices: [4], ans: 0-3, exp, src}
const QB_AAMC_CHEM = [
  {s:'Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'Which pair of molecules can hydrogen-bond to each other with EACH molecule able to act as a hydrogen-bond donor?',choices:['Methane and ammonia','Carbon dioxide and methanol','Methanol and ammonia','Dimethyl ether and acetone'],ans:2,exp:'A hydrogen-bond **donor** needs H bonded to N, O or F; both methanol (O–H) and ammonia (N–H) have one, so each can donate and accept. Carbon dioxide and ethers or ketones only accept (lone pairs on O), and methane has only C–H bonds.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q1'},
  {s:'Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'Carbon dioxide has two polar C=O bonds, yet the molecule is nonpolar because:',choices:['its linear shape makes the two bond dipoles cancel','carbon and oxygen have nearly equal electronegativities','its bent shape makes the two bond dipoles cancel','its central carbon carries two lone pairs'],ans:0,exp:'CO₂ is **linear** (two electron domains, no lone pairs on C), so its equal and opposite bond dipoles sum to zero. Contrast water, which is bent and therefore polar; the O–C electronegativity difference (about 1.0) does make each bond polar.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q1b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'What is the pH of a 0.001 M solution of nitric acid?',choices:['2','3','4','11'],ans:1,exp:'HNO₃ is a **strong acid**, so it dissociates completely and [H⁺] = 1×10⁻³ M; pH = −log(10⁻³) = 3. A pH of 11 would be the answer for 0.001 M of a strong base.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q2'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'At 25 °C, what is [OH⁻] in a solution whose pH is 4?',choices:['1×10⁻⁴ M','1×10⁻⁷ M','1×10⁻¹⁴ M','1×10⁻¹⁰ M'],ans:3,exp:'pOH = 14 − pH = 10, so [OH⁻] = 1×10⁻¹⁰ M. Equivalently, [H⁺][OH⁻] = **Kw = 1×10⁻¹⁴**, and 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 10⁻⁴ = 10⁻¹⁰; 1×10⁻⁴ M is [H⁺], not [OH⁻].',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q2b'},
  {s:'Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'Which molecule has the same molecular geometry as methane?',choices:['SF₄','XeF₄','SiH₄','BF₃'],ans:2,exp:'SiH₄ has four bonding pairs and no lone pairs on Si, giving a **tetrahedral** shape like CH₄. SF₄ is seesaw (one lone pair), XeF₄ is square planar (two lone pairs), and BF₃ is trigonal planar (three domains).',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q3'},
  {s:'Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'What are the electron-domain geometry and molecular geometry of NH₃, respectively?',choices:['tetrahedral / trigonal pyramidal','trigonal planar / trigonal planar','tetrahedral / tetrahedral','trigonal pyramidal / bent'],ans:0,exp:'N has four electron domains (three bonds and one lone pair), so the **electron geometry** is tetrahedral, but the **molecular geometry** describes only atom positions: trigonal pyramidal. The lone pair also compresses the H–N–H angle to about 107°.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q3b'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Which element most readily accepts an additional electron (has the most favorable electron affinity)?',choices:['Argon','Sodium','Magnesium','Sulfur'],ans:3,exp:'Sulfur, a group 16 nonmetal like oxygen, is two electrons short of an octet and releases energy on gaining an electron. Noble gases such as argon and the filled-subshell atom magnesium have unfavorable electron affinities, and sodium, a metal, tends to lose electrons.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q4'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry & Redox',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'In a redox reaction, the oxidizing agent is the species that:',choices:['loses electrons and is oxidized','gains electrons and is reduced','gains electrons and is oxidized','loses electrons and is reduced'],ans:1,exp:'An **oxidizing agent** causes another species to be oxidized by taking its electrons, so the oxidizing agent itself is reduced. Mnemonic **OIL RIG**: oxidation is loss, reduction is gain; the reducing agent is the one oxidized.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q4b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5C',q:'A mixture of a hydrocarbon and a ketone of similar molar mass is distilled. Which statement correctly predicts the outcome?',choices:['The ketone distills first, because dipole forces raise its vapor pressure','The hydrocarbon distills first, because it can hydrogen-bond with itself','The hydrocarbon distills first, because only London forces hold it together','The ketone distills first, because its C=O bond lowers its boiling point'],ans:2,exp:'The hydrocarbon has only weak **London dispersion forces**, so it has the higher vapor pressure and lower boiling point and is collected first. The polar C=O gives the ketone dipole–dipole attractions, which lower its vapor pressure; neither compound can hydrogen-bond with itself.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q5'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5C',q:'Fractional rather than simple distillation is preferred when two miscible liquids:',choices:['have boiling points within about 25 °C of each other','have boiling points that differ by more than 100 °C','are immiscible and form two separate layers','include one component that decomposes when heated'],ans:0,exp:'Close boiling points need the repeated vaporization–condensation cycles of a **fractionating column**; widely separated boiling points can be separated by simple distillation. Heat-sensitive compounds call for **vacuum distillation**, and immiscible layers are separated with a separatory funnel.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q5b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5C',q:'Boiling chips, or a fine stream of air bubbles in a vacuum distillation, are used in order to:',choices:['lower the boiling point of the liquid by adding impurities','provide nucleation sites so the liquid boils smoothly','absorb water so that the distillate stays dry','catalyze evaporation of the higher-boiling component'],ans:1,exp:'Bubbles give vapor a place to form, preventing **superheating** and sudden violent boiling (**bumping**). Under vacuum, boiling chips work poorly, so an air bleed (capillary) or stirring is used for the same purpose; neither changes the boiling point.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q6'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5C',q:'A smooth liquid heated above its boiling point without forming bubbles, which may then erupt violently, is described as:',choices:['supercooled','supersaturated','azeotropic','superheated'],ans:3,exp:'A **superheated** liquid lacks nucleation sites, so vapor cannot form until a sudden burst (bumping). Supercooled describes a liquid below its freezing point, and supersaturated describes a solution holding more solute than its equilibrium solubility.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q6b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5C',q:'Which change would most improve the separation achieved in a fractional distillation?',choices:['Heating the flask more slowly','Heating the flask more rapidly','Using a shorter fractionating column','Removing the packing from the column'],ans:0,exp:'Slow heating lets vapor and liquid approach **equilibrium** at each level of the column, so each vaporization–condensation cycle enriches the more volatile component. Fast heating, a shorter column or less packing all reduce the number of effective cycles.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q7'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5C',q:'A longer or more densely packed fractionating column gives better separation mainly because it:',choices:['raises the boiling point of the more volatile component','lowers the pressure above the boiling liquid','provides more theoretical plates for vapor–liquid cycles','prevents bumping of the boiling mixture'],ans:2,exp:'Each **theoretical plate** is one vaporization–condensation equilibrium, and every cycle further enriches the vapor in the lower-boiling liquid. Column length does not change boiling points or the system pressure; bumping is controlled with boiling chips or stirring.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q7b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'Which compound contains a chiral carbon?',choices:['2-Propanol','1-Butanol','2-Methyl-2-propanol','2-Butanol'],ans:3,exp:'C-2 of 2-butanol bears four different groups (H, OH, CH₃ and CH₂CH₃), making it a **stereocenter**. In 2-propanol C-2 carries two identical methyls, 2-methyl-2-propanol has three methyls on its carbinol carbon, and 1-butanol has no carbon with four different groups.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q8'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'Two enantiomers of a compound with a single stereocenter will differ in their:',choices:['boiling points','direction of optical rotation','molecular formulas','solubility in water'],ans:1,exp:'Enantiomers are nonsuperimposable mirror images with identical physical properties in an achiral environment, except that they rotate plane-polarized light by **equal magnitudes in opposite directions**. They can only be separated by distillation or crystallization after conversion to diastereomers.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q8b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5C',q:'During a vacuum distillation, a leak lets the pressure inside the apparatus rise. The boiling point of the liquid will:',choices:['decrease, because molecules escape the liquid more easily','stay the same, because boiling point is an intrinsic property','increase, because a higher vapor pressure is now needed to boil','decrease, because vapor is removed from the flask more quickly'],ans:2,exp:'A liquid boils when its **vapor pressure equals the external pressure**; raising the external pressure means a higher temperature is needed to reach it. Only the normal boiling point refers to a fixed pressure of 1 atm.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q9'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5C',q:'Vacuum distillation is the method of choice for purifying a compound that:',choices:['has a high boiling point and decomposes near it at 1 atm','boils within a few degrees of a contaminant','forms a separate layer from water','boils well below room temperature at 1 atm'],ans:0,exp:'Lowering the pressure lowers the boiling point, so **high-boiling or heat-sensitive** compounds can be distilled without decomposing. Close-boiling mixtures need fractional distillation, and immiscible layers are separated by extraction.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q9b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'How many sodium ions are present in 0.10 mol of Na₂CO₃?',choices:['6.0×10²²','1.2×10²³','6.0×10²³','1.8×10²³'],ans:1,exp:'Each formula unit releases **two Na⁺**, so 0.10 mol Na₂CO₃ gives 0.20 mol Na⁺; 0.20 × 6.02×10²³ = 1.2×10²³ ions. Counting all three ions per formula unit would give 1.8×10²³.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q10'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'How many moles of CuSO₄·5H₂O (molar mass ≈ 250 g/mol) are in 25.0 g of the hydrate?',choices:['0.157 mol','0.500 mol','0.100 mol','0.0200 mol'],ans:2,exp:'The molar mass of a **hydrate** includes its water of crystallization: 159.6 + 5(18.0) ≈ 250 g/mol, so 25.0 g ÷ 250 g/mol = 0.100 mol. Dividing by the anhydrous mass (159.6 g/mol) gives the tempting but wrong 0.157 mol.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q10b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'An aqueous solution of sodium carbonate turns red litmus blue because:',choices:['sodium ions react with water to form NaOH','carbonate decomposes to release CO₂ gas','sodium ions accept protons from water','carbonate accepts a proton from water, forming OH⁻'],ans:3,exp:'CO₃²⁻ is the conjugate base of the weak acid HCO₃⁻, so it **hydrolyzes**: CO₃²⁻ + H₂O ⇌ HCO₃⁻ + OH⁻. Na⁺, the cation of a strong base, is a spectator ion with no effect on pH.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q11'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Which salt produces a neutral aqueous solution?',choices:['NH₄Cl','KNO₃','NaF','K₂CO₃'],ans:1,exp:'KNO₃ comes from a strong base (KOH) and a strong acid (HNO₃), so neither ion hydrolyzes. NH₄Cl is acidic (NH₄⁺ is a weak acid), while NaF and K₂CO₃ are basic because F⁻ and CO₃²⁻ are **conjugate bases of weak acids**.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q11b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'A solution containing 0.040 mol Ni²⁺ is mixed with 50.0 mL of 0.50 M Na₂CO₃. What is the maximum amount of NiCO₃ that can precipitate?',choices:['0.015 mol','0.040 mol','0.065 mol','0.025 mol'],ans:3,exp:'The carbonate solution supplies 0.0500 L × 0.50 M = 0.025 mol CO₃²⁻. Ni²⁺ and CO₃²⁻ combine 1:1, so carbonate is the **limiting reagent** and caps the yield at 0.025 mol NiCO₃, leaving 0.015 mol Ni²⁺ in excess. Adding the two amounts (0.065 mol) ignores the stoichiometry.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q12'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'For 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, a mixture of 4 mol H₂ and 3 mol O₂ reacts completely. Which statement is correct?',choices:['H₂ is limiting, and 4 mol H₂O forms','O₂ is limiting, and 6 mol H₂O forms','H₂ is limiting, and 3 mol H₂O forms','O₂ is limiting, and 3 mol H₂O forms'],ans:0,exp:'4 mol H₂ needs only 2 mol O₂, so H₂ runs out first (**limiting reagent**) and produces 4 mol H₂O, leaving 1 mol O₂ in excess. Compare mole ratios to the coefficients rather than the raw amounts.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q12b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'When hydrochloric acid is added to solid calcium carbonate, the solid dissolves and which gas is released?',choices:['H₂','CO₂','O₂','Cl₂'],ans:1,exp:'H⁺ protonates carbonate to form **carbonic acid**, which decomposes: H₂CO₃ → H₂O + CO₂(g). H₂ gas comes from acids reacting with active metals, not with carbonates.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q13'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Hyperventilation removes CO₂ from the blood. Based on CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂CO₃ ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻, how does blood pH change?',choices:['It falls, because H₂CO₃ accumulates','It rises, because the equilibrium shifts toward CO₂ and consumes H⁺','It falls, because more HCO₃⁻ dissociates','It stays the same, because the bicarbonate buffer fully compensates'],ans:1,exp:'Removing CO₂ shifts the equilibrium left by **Le Châtelier**, using up H⁺ and raising pH (respiratory alkalosis). Hypoventilation does the reverse, retaining CO₂ and lowering pH.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q13b'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Aqueous Cu²⁺ is colored while aqueous Zn²⁺ is colorless. This is because Cu²⁺:',choices:['has a completely filled d subshell','has no electrons in its d subshell','has a larger ionic radius than Zn²⁺','has a partially filled d subshell'],ans:3,exp:'Cu²⁺ is d⁹, so electrons can undergo **d–d transitions** that absorb visible light; the solution shows the complementary color. Zn²⁺ is d¹⁰, with no vacancy in the d subshell, so it absorbs no visible light.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q14'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'A Cu(OH)₂ precipitate dissolves when excess aqueous ammonia is added. The best explanation is that:',choices:['NH₃ forms the soluble complex ion [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺','NH₃ lowers the pH enough to dissolve the hydroxide','NH₃ oxidizes Cu²⁺ to a more soluble ion','NH₃ raises the Ksp of Cu(OH)₂'],ans:0,exp:'NH₃ acts as a **Lewis base** ligand, binding Cu²⁺ in a deep blue ammine complex; removing free Cu²⁺ pulls the dissolution equilibrium forward. Ammonia actually raises pH, and Ksp is constant at a given temperature.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q14b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'2-Hydroxybenzoic acid (pKa ≈ 3.0) is a stronger acid than 4-hydroxybenzoic acid (pKa ≈ 4.5). The best explanation is that in the 2-isomer:',choices:['the hydroxyl proton is the one that ionizes first','the carboxylate is stabilized by an intramolecular hydrogen bond to the adjacent OH','the un-ionized acid is stabilized by hydrogen bonding to the ring','the ring is more electron-rich than in the 4-isomer'],ans:1,exp:'The neighboring OH donates a hydrogen bond to the carboxylate oxygen, **stabilizing the conjugate base** and favoring ionization. Any factor that stabilizes the anion (resonance, induction, hydrogen bonding) strengthens the acid; stabilizing the un-ionized acid would weaken it.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q15'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'Which carboxylic acid is the strongest?',choices:['Acetic acid','Chloroacetic acid','Trifluoroacetic acid','Propanoic acid'],ans:2,exp:'Three strongly electronegative fluorines withdraw electron density by **induction**, dispersing the negative charge of the carboxylate (pKa ≈ 0.2). One chlorine has a smaller effect, and alkyl groups in propanoic acid slightly destabilize the anion.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q15b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'In a drawing of a stereocenter, swapping the positions of any two substituents changes an R configuration to:',choices:['S, because one swap inverts the configuration','R, because the same four groups are present','an achiral center, because symmetry is created','a meso form, because a mirror plane appears'],ans:0,exp:'A **single interchange** of two groups produces the mirror-image configuration (R ↔ S); two interchanges restore the original. This is a quick way to compare wedge-dash or Fischer drawings without rebuilding models.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q16'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'Rank these groups on a stereocenter by Cahn–Ingold–Prelog priority, highest first: –COOH, –OH, –CH₂OH, –H.',choices:['–COOH > –OH > –CH₂OH > –H','–OH > –COOH > –CH₂OH > –H','–COOH > –CH₂OH > –OH > –H','–OH > –CH₂OH > –COOH > –H'],ans:1,exp:'Priority goes by atomic number of the first attached atom, so O (in –OH) beats C. Between the two carbons, –COOH has (O,O,O) substituents versus (O,H,H) for –CH₂OH, so **–COOH ranks higher**; H is always lowest.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q16b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'Complete saponification of one molecule of a triacylglycerol with NaOH yields:',choices:['glycerol and one fatty acid salt','ethylene glycol and two fatty acid salts','glycerol and three fatty acid salts','three alcohols and one glycerate salt'],ans:2,exp:'A triacylglycerol is a **triester** of glycerol; base hydrolysis cleaves all three ester bonds, releasing glycerol and three carboxylate (soap) anions. The ratio of different fatty acid salts reveals how many of each acyl chain the original fat carried.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q17'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'Acid-catalyzed hydrolysis of ethyl acetate produces:',choices:['acetaldehyde and ethanol','acetic acid and ethene','formic acid and propanol','acetic acid and ethanol'],ans:3,exp:'Hydrolysis breaks the ester C(=O)–O bond, giving back the parent **carboxylic acid and alcohol**; it is the reverse of Fischer esterification. Under acid it is reversible, so excess water drives it to completion.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q17b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5C',q:'Which compound of similar molar mass is most soluble in water?',choices:['Pentane','Diethylamine','Cyclopentane','1-Pentene'],ans:1,exp:'The amine N–H and nitrogen lone pair let diethylamine **hydrogen-bond with water**, making it miscible. Pentane, cyclopentane and 1-pentene are hydrocarbons with only London forces and are essentially insoluble.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q18'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5C',q:'A tertiary amine boils lower than a primary amine of similar molar mass because the tertiary amine:',choices:['has no lone pair on nitrogen','is ionized in the pure liquid','has stronger London dispersion forces','has no N–H bond to donate hydrogen bonds'],ans:3,exp:'Without an N–H, tertiary amine molecules cannot **hydrogen-bond to each other**, though they can still accept hydrogen bonds from water. The nitrogen lone pair is still present, which is why tertiary amines remain basic.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q18b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5C',q:'A ketone is reduced to an alcohol and both are run on a silica gel TLC plate with a fairly nonpolar eluent. Compared with the ketone, the alcohol shows:',choices:['a lower Rf, because it binds the polar silica more strongly','a higher Rf, because it is more polar','the same Rf, because their molar masses are nearly equal','a lower Rf, because it is less polar'],ans:0,exp:'Silica is a **polar stationary phase**; the alcohol O–H hydrogen-bonds to it, so the alcohol travels less far and has a smaller Rf. In reverse-phase chromatography (nonpolar stationary phase) the order would flip.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q19'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'Treating 2-butanone with NaBH₄ followed by aqueous workup gives:',choices:['1-butanol','butanal','2-butanol','butanoic acid'],ans:2,exp:'NaBH₄ delivers **hydride** to the carbonyl carbon, reducing ketones to secondary alcohols and aldehydes to primary alcohols. It is too mild to reduce esters or carboxylic acids, and it never moves the oxygen to a different carbon.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q19b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'What mass of NaCl (58.4 g/mol) is contained in 25.0 mL of a 0.200 M NaCl solution?',choices:['0.146 g','0.292 g','1.17 g','2.92 g'],ans:1,exp:'Moles = M × V = 0.200 mol/L × 0.0250 L = 5.00×10⁻³ mol; mass = 5.00×10⁻³ × 58.4 ≈ **0.292 g**. Forgetting to convert mL to L gives 292 g, an obvious red flag.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q20'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'10.0 mL of a 2.0 M stock solution is diluted with water to a final volume of 50.0 mL. What is the new concentration?',choices:['0.20 M','1.0 M','10 M','0.40 M'],ans:3,exp:'Using **M₁V₁ = M₂V₂**: (2.0 M)(10.0 mL) = M₂(50.0 mL), so M₂ = 0.40 M. Dilution keeps moles of solute constant while volume increases fivefold.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q20b'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'An enzyme-catalyzed reaction has reached equilibrium. Which change will increase the final amount of product?',choices:['Adding more enzyme','Adding a cofactor that speeds the enzyme','Removing a product as it forms','Using an enzyme with a higher turnover number'],ans:2,exp:'Removing a product lowers Q below K, so by **Le Châtelier** the reaction shifts forward to make more product. Enzymes and cofactors are catalysts: they speed both directions equally and change how fast equilibrium is reached, not where it lies.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q21'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'Adding a catalyst to a reaction mixture at constant temperature has what effect?',choices:['K increases, so more product forms','K is unchanged, but equilibrium is reached faster','K decreases, so less product forms','K is unchanged, but more product forms'],ans:1,exp:'A catalyst lowers the **activation energy** of both the forward and reverse paths by the same amount, so K and the equilibrium composition are unchanged. Only a temperature change alters the value of K.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q21b'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'What is the equilibrium expression for 2A(aq) ⇌ B(aq) + 3C(aq)?',choices:['[B][C]³ / [A]²','[A]² / [B][C]³','[B][3C] / [2A]','[B] + [C]³ / [A]²'],ans:0,exp:'K = **products over reactants**, each concentration raised to its stoichiometric coefficient, and multiplied rather than added. Coefficients become exponents, never multipliers inside the brackets.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q22'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'What is the equilibrium expression for CaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g)?',choices:['K = [CaO][CO₂] / [CaCO₃]','K = [CaCO₃] / [CaO][CO₂]','K = [CO₂]','K = [CaO] / [CaCO₃]'],ans:2,exp:'**Pure solids and liquids** have activities of 1 and are omitted, leaving only the gas: K = [CO₂] (or P of CO₂). This is why the CO₂ pressure over heated limestone is fixed at a given temperature regardless of how much solid is present.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q22b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Each polymer chain of 50 monomer units releases one phosphate ion per unit added. If 2.0 mM phosphate is produced, what is the concentration of polymer chains?',choices:['2.0 mM','100 mM','0.020 mM','0.040 mM'],ans:3,exp:'50 phosphates are released per chain, so [chains] = 2.0 mM ÷ 50 = **0.040 mM**. Multiplying instead of dividing (100 mM) is the classic error; always set up the mole ratio explicitly.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q23'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'For N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, how many moles of NH₃ can form from 6 mol H₂ with excess N₂?',choices:['4 mol','2 mol','3 mol','6 mol'],ans:0,exp:'The **mole ratio** NH₃ : H₂ is 2 : 3, so 6 mol H₂ × (2/3) = 4 mol NH₃. Mass is conserved, but moles are not necessarily, so 6 mol is wrong.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q23b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'A weak acid with pKa = 4.7 is in a solution buffered at pH 6.7. What is the ratio [A⁻]:[HA]?',choices:['1:100','2:1','100:1','20:1'],ans:2,exp:'**Henderson–Hasselbalch**: pH − pKa = log([A⁻]/[HA]) = 2, so the ratio is 10² = 100:1. When pH is above pKa the deprotonated form dominates; 1:100 is the inverted ratio.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q24'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'A weak acid and its conjugate base buffer most effectively when the pH is:',choices:['at least 2 units above the pKa','within about 1 unit of the pKa','exactly 7, regardless of the pKa','equal to the pKb of the conjugate base'],ans:1,exp:'Within pKa ± 1, both HA and A⁻ are present in substantial amounts (ratio between 1:10 and 10:1), so the buffer can neutralize added acid or base. Buffering is maximal at **pH = pKa**.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q24b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'Which reagent is used to cleave the ester bonds of a fat in saponification?',choices:['Sodium chloride','Sodium sulfate','Sodium nitrate','Sodium hydroxide'],ans:3,exp:'**Hydroxide** is the nucleophile that attacks the ester carbonyl, releasing glycerol and carboxylate salts. Neutral salts such as NaCl provide no nucleophilic base (NaCl is used afterward to salt out the soap).',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q25'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'Heating methyl acetate in excess ethanol with an acid catalyst (transesterification) produces:',choices:['ethyl acetate and methanol','acetic acid and dimethyl ether','ethyl methyl ether and acetic acid','acetaldehyde and methanol'],ans:0,exp:'In **transesterification** an alcohol replaces the alkoxy group of an ester: ethanol displaces methoxide, giving ethyl acetate and methanol. Excess ethanol drives the reversible exchange forward; this is how biodiesel is made from fats.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q25b'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'Burning a food sample warms 500 g of water by 20 °C (c = 1 cal/g·°C). How many food Calories did the sample release, assuming all heat went into the water?',choices:['1 Cal','10 Cal','100 Cal','10,000 Cal'],ans:1,exp:'q = mcΔT = 500 g × 1 cal/g·°C × 20 °C = 10,000 cal = 10 kcal. One **food Calorie** (capital C) equals 1 kcal, so the sample released 10 Cal; 10,000 confuses calories with Calories.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q26'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'How much heat is needed to warm 200 g of water by 5.0 °C? (c = 4.18 J/g·°C)',choices:['0.84 kJ','1.0 kJ','4.2 kJ','42 kJ'],ans:2,exp:'q = mcΔT = 200 × 4.18 × 5.0 = 4180 J ≈ **4.2 kJ**. Leaving out ΔT gives 0.84 kJ, and using 1 cal/g·°C gives 1000 cal, which must still be converted (1 cal = 4.184 J) rather than read as 1.0 kJ.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q26b'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'A synthetic fat substitute has ester bonds that digestive lipases cannot reach, so it passes through the gut unabsorbed. About how many Calories per gram does it provide?',choices:['4 Cal/g','7 Cal/g','9 Cal/g','0 Cal/g'],ans:3,exp:'Energy is only obtained from food that is **hydrolyzed and absorbed**; an indigestible fat contributes essentially nothing. A digestible fat with the same structure would supply about 9 Cal/g.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q27'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'Using standard values, about how many Calories are in a snack with 10 g carbohydrate and 5 g fat?',choices:['85 Cal','60 Cal','65 Cal','135 Cal'],ans:0,exp:'Carbohydrate and protein give about **4 Cal/g** and fat about **9 Cal/g**: 10 × 4 + 5 × 9 = 40 + 45 = 85 Cal. Applying 4 Cal/g to everything gives 60 Cal, and 9 Cal/g to everything gives 135 Cal.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q27b'},
  {s:'Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'Glycerol (propane-1,2,3-triol) boils roughly 200 °C higher than 2-propanol. The main reason is that glycerol:',choices:['has a more branched carbon skeleton','forms more hydrogen bonds per molecule','is ionized in the liquid state','has weaker London dispersion forces'],ans:1,exp:'Glycerol has **three OH groups**, so each molecule participates in many more hydrogen bonds than 2-propanol, which has one. More intermolecular attraction means more energy is needed to reach the vapor; neither alcohol is ionized.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q28'},
  {s:'Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'Which of these compounds of similar molar mass has the highest boiling point?',choices:['Butane','Propanal','1-Propanol','Ethylene glycol'],ans:3,exp:'Ethylene glycol (HOCH₂CH₂OH) has **two OH groups** for hydrogen bonding and boils near 197 °C. 1-Propanol has one OH (≈97 °C), propanal has only dipole forces (≈48 °C), and butane has only London forces (≈ −1 °C).',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q28b'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Which period 3 element has the largest atomic radius?',choices:['Magnesium','Silicon','Phosphorus','Chlorine'],ans:0,exp:'Atomic radius **decreases left to right** across a period, so magnesium, furthest left of these, is largest. Chlorine is the smallest because its valence electrons feel the highest effective nuclear charge.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q29'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Atomic radius decreases across a period mainly because:',choices:['electrons are added to new, lower-energy shells','shielding by inner electrons increases sharply','effective nuclear charge increases while shielding stays similar','the number of neutrons decreases'],ans:2,exp:'Across a period each added proton raises nuclear charge, but electrons added to the **same shell** shield each other poorly, so **Z_eff** rises and pulls the valence shell inward. Down a group, by contrast, new shells make atoms larger.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q29b'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Which period 3 element has the highest first ionization energy?',choices:['Sodium','Phosphorus','Argon','Chlorine'],ans:2,exp:'The **noble gas** at the end of a period has a full valence shell and the highest Z_eff in that period, so removing an electron costs the most energy. Sodium, with one valence electron, has the lowest.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q30'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'The second ionization energy of sodium is about ten times its first. Why?',choices:['The second electron comes from a filled inner (2p) shell','The second electron is removed from a 3p orbital','Na⁺ has a smaller nuclear charge than Na','The second electron is farther from the nucleus'],ans:0,exp:'After losing its single 3s electron, Na⁺ has a **noble-gas (neon) core**; the next electron must come from the much more tightly held 2p subshell. Large jumps between successive ionization energies reveal the number of valence electrons.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q30b'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'How many neutrons are in a neutral atom of cesium-137 (Z = 55)?',choices:['55','137','82','192'],ans:2,exp:'Neutrons = **mass number − atomic number** = 137 − 55 = 82. The neutral atom also has 55 protons and 55 electrons; 192 is the mass number plus the atomic number.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q31'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Isotopes of the same element differ in their:',choices:['number of protons','number of electrons','number of valence electrons','number of neutrons'],ans:3,exp:'**Isotopes** share an atomic number (protons) and therefore chemistry, but have different neutron counts and mass numbers. Differences in electron number would describe ions, not isotopes.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q31b'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Which pair of elements is most likely to form an ionic compound?',choices:['Carbon and oxygen','Potassium and bromine','Nitrogen and hydrogen','Sulfur and chlorine'],ans:1,exp:'A metal from the far left (K) and a nonmetal from the far right (Br) have a **large electronegativity difference**, so an electron is transferred to form K⁺ and Br⁻. The other pairs are two nonmetals, which share electrons covalently.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q32'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Moseley showed that the properties of the elements are periodic functions of their:',choices:['atomic mass','neutron number','atomic number','density'],ans:2,exp:'X-ray studies by Moseley tied each element to its nuclear charge, the **atomic number**, which fixed anomalies in mass-based ordering (for example Te before I, and Ar before K). The modern periodic law is based on atomic number.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q32b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'The steroid ring system consists of:',choices:['three six-membered rings and one five-membered ring','four six-membered rings','two six-membered rings and two five-membered rings','three five-membered rings and one six-membered ring'],ans:0,exp:'Steroids share a fused **6-6-6-5** tetracyclic skeleton (rings A, B, C and D). Cholesterol, cortisol, testosterone and estradiol all derive from it; substituents above the ring plane are β (wedge) and below are α (dash).',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q33'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'In the most stable chair conformation of a monosubstituted cyclohexane, a bulky substituent occupies:',choices:['an axial position, to avoid 1,3-diaxial strain','an equatorial position, to avoid 1,3-diaxial strain','an axial position, to minimize ring strain','an equatorial position, to maximize torsional strain'],ans:1,exp:'An **equatorial** substituent points away from the ring and avoids steric clashes with the axial hydrogens on carbons 3 and 5 (**1,3-diaxial interactions**). The larger the group, as with tert-butyl, the stronger the equatorial preference.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q33b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'Alcohols undergo nucleophilic substitution with HBr but not with NaBr. Why is the acid required?',choices:['Acid makes bromide a stronger nucleophile','Acid removes water so the reaction is not reversible','Protonating –OH turns it into water, a good leaving group','Protonating –OH makes the carbon less electrophilic'],ans:2,exp:'Hydroxide is a strong base and a **poor leaving group**; protonation converts –OH into –OH₂⁺, which leaves as neutral water. Good leaving groups are weak bases, the conjugate bases of strong acids.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q34'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'Which reagent converts 1-butanol into 1-chlorobutane?',choices:['SOCl₂','NaBH₄','PCC','KMnO₄'],ans:0,exp:'**Thionyl chloride** turns the OH into a chlorosulfite leaving group that chloride displaces, releasing SO₂ and HCl. NaBH₄ is a reducing agent, while PCC and KMnO₄ oxidize alcohols to carbonyl compounds.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q34b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'4D',q:'An aldehyde on a phenol ring is reduced to a primary alcohol. Which IR change best confirms the reduction?',choices:['Appearance of a broad peak near 3300 cm⁻¹','Appearance of a sharp peak near 2250 cm⁻¹','Appearance of a peak near 1650 cm⁻¹','Disappearance of the strong peak near 1700 cm⁻¹'],ans:3,exp:'Loss of the **C=O stretch** (about 1700 cm⁻¹) shows the carbonyl is gone. A broad O–H band was already present from the phenol, so its appearance would not be diagnostic; 2250 cm⁻¹ signals a nitrile.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q35'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'4D',q:'A broad, strong IR absorption from about 3200 to 3550 cm⁻¹ most likely indicates:',choices:['a carbonyl C=O stretch','an alcohol O–H stretch','a nitrile C≡N stretch','an alkene C=C stretch'],ans:1,exp:'Hydrogen bonding broadens the **alcohol O–H** stretch into a wide band near 3300 cm⁻¹. A carboxylic acid O–H is even broader (2500–3300 cm⁻¹), whereas C=O appears sharp near 1700 cm⁻¹.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q35b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'Which species is a strong base but a poor nucleophile?',choices:['Sodium methoxide','Sodium iodide','Potassium tert-butoxide','Sodium cyanide'],ans:2,exp:'The bulky tert-butyl groups **sterically block** the oxygen from attacking a carbon, but it can still remove an accessible proton, so it favors E2 over SN2. Basicity is a thermodynamic property, while nucleophilicity is kinetic and sensitive to steric hindrance.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q36'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'In a polar protic solvent such as water, which halide ion is the best nucleophile?',choices:['F⁻','Cl⁻','Br⁻','I⁻'],ans:3,exp:'Protic solvents **hydrogen-bond tightly to small anions** like F⁻, caging them; large, polarizable I⁻ is weakly solvated and reacts fastest. In polar aprotic solvents the order reverses toward basicity, with F⁻ most nucleophilic.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q36b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'How many different tetrapeptide sequences can be made from four different amino acids if each is used exactly once?',choices:['4','12','256','24'],ans:3,exp:'Without repetition, the number of orderings is **4! = 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 24**. Sequence order matters because peptides have distinct N- and C-termini; 256 (4⁴) would allow each amino acid to repeat.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q37'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'By convention, the tripeptide Ala-Gly-Ser has which group at the left end of its name?',choices:['a free α-amino group on alanine','a free α-carboxyl group on alanine','a free α-amino group on serine','a free α-carboxyl group on serine'],ans:0,exp:'Peptides are written from the **N-terminus to the C-terminus**, so alanine carries the free amino group and serine the free carboxyl group. Ala-Gly-Ser and Ser-Gly-Ala are therefore different molecules.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q37b'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Sulfur hexafluoride (SF₆) is best classified as:',choices:['an ionic compound','a network covalent solid','a covalent molecular compound','a metallic compound'],ans:2,exp:'S and F are both nonmetals, so they **share electrons**, forming discrete SF₆ molecules held together only by weak London forces, which is why it is a gas and does not conduct electricity. Ionic compounds form between metals and nonmetals.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q38'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Which substance conducts electricity in the solid state?',choices:['Sodium chloride','Copper','Sucrose','Ice'],ans:1,exp:'Metals conduct as solids because of **delocalized (mobile) electrons**. Solid NaCl has ions locked in a lattice and conducts only when molten or dissolved; molecular solids such as sucrose and ice do not conduct.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q38b'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Most nonmetals are located in which region of the periodic table?',choices:['The lower left','The d block','The f block','The upper right'],ans:3,exp:'Nonmetals (for example N, O, F, the halogens and noble gases) sit in the **upper right**, with hydrogen as the exception on the left. Metals fill the left side, the d block and the f block.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q39'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Which element is a metalloid?',choices:['Silicon','Aluminum','Sulfur','Phosphorus'],ans:0,exp:'**Metalloids** (B, Si, Ge, As, Sb, Te) lie along the staircase dividing metals from nonmetals and have intermediate properties, such as silicon\'s semiconductivity. Aluminum is a metal, while sulfur and phosphorus are nonmetals.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q39b'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Which compound is ionic?',choices:['HCl','CH₄','KCl','NO₂'],ans:2,exp:'KCl pairs a group 1 metal with a halogen, so electrons transfer to form K⁺ and Cl⁻ ions. HCl, CH₄ and NO₂ consist only of nonmetals and are **covalent molecules**.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q40'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Which set of properties is typical of ionic compounds?',choices:['Low melting points and conductivity as solids','High melting points and conductivity when molten','Low melting points and no conductivity when molten','High melting points and conductivity as solids'],ans:1,exp:'Strong electrostatic attraction in the **crystal lattice** gives high melting points, and ions become mobile, and so conduct, only when melted or dissolved. Solid ionic compounds are brittle insulators.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q40b'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Which compound has the greatest ionic character?',choices:['LiI','CsI','LiF','CsF'],ans:3,exp:'Ionic character grows with the **electronegativity difference**. Cs is the least electronegative of these metals (EN decreases down a group) and F is the most electronegative element, so CsF has the largest difference.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q41'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Electronegativity generally:',choices:['increases across a period and decreases down a group','decreases across a period and increases down a group','increases across a period and down a group','decreases across a period and down a group'],ans:0,exp:'Higher **effective nuclear charge** across a period and smaller atomic size make atoms pull bonding electrons more strongly; down a group, larger radius weakens that pull. Fluorine is the most electronegative element.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q41b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4C',q:'HCl is a covalent molecule, yet its aqueous solution conducts electricity well. Why?',choices:['HCl molecules carry a net charge in water','Water itself becomes a strong conductor when acid is added','HCl ionizes in water to form H₃O⁺ and Cl⁻ ions','HCl contains ionic bonds that become visible in water'],ans:2,exp:'Conductivity requires **mobile ions**; HCl, a strong acid, transfers its proton to water and ionizes completely, making it a strong electrolyte. Conductivity in solution therefore does not prove that the pure compound is ionic.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q42'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4C',q:'Which 0.10 m aqueous solution has the lowest freezing point?',choices:['NaCl','Glucose','KBr','CaCl₂'],ans:3,exp:'Freezing-point depression is a **colligative property**, ΔT = i·Kf·m, depending on the number of dissolved particles. CaCl₂ gives three ions (i ≈ 3), NaCl and KBr give two, and glucose gives one.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q42b'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Which element forms a network solid with the same tetrahedral, diamond-like bonding as carbon?',choices:['Silicon','Phosphorus','Sulfur','Aluminum'],ans:0,exp:'Silicon is in **group 14** with carbon, so it has four valence electrons and forms four tetrahedral sp³ bonds in a diamond-cubic lattice (as does germanium). Elements in the same group share bonding patterns because they have the same valence configuration.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q43'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Elements in the same group of the periodic table have similar chemical properties mainly because they have the same:',choices:['atomic radius','number of valence electrons','number of electron shells','mass number'],ans:1,exp:'Chemistry is governed by the **valence electron configuration**, which repeats down a group (for example ns¹ for the alkali metals). Elements in the same period share the number of occupied shells, not their properties.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q43b'},
  {s:'Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'Melting a network covalent solid such as quartz (SiO₂) requires breaking:',choices:['hydrogen bonds between molecules','London forces between molecules','covalent bonds throughout the lattice','ionic bonds between ions'],ans:2,exp:'A **network solid** is one giant molecule held together by covalent bonds in every direction, so melting means breaking strong bonds, giving very high melting points. Molecular solids melt by overcoming only weak intermolecular forces.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q44'},
  {s:'Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'When liquid methanol boils, which interactions are overcome?',choices:['Hydrogen bonds between molecules','O–H covalent bonds within molecules','C–O covalent bonds within molecules','Ionic attractions between molecules'],ans:0,exp:'Phase changes of molecular substances overcome **intermolecular forces** only; methanol vapor still consists of intact CH₃OH molecules. Breaking covalent bonds would be a chemical change.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q44b'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'When a gas deposits directly as an ordered crystalline solid, the entropy of the system:',choices:['increases','stays constant','becomes zero','decreases'],ans:3,exp:'Going from freely moving gas molecules to a fixed lattice greatly reduces the number of accessible microstates, so **ΔS < 0**. Entropy reaches zero only for a perfect crystal at 0 K.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q45'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'Which process has a positive entropy change for the system?',choices:['H₂O(g) → H₂O(l)','N₂O₄(g) → 2NO₂(g)','NH₃(g) + HCl(g) → NH₄Cl(s)','2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(l)'],ans:1,exp:'Entropy rises when the **number of gas molecules increases** (1 → 2 here). The other processes condense gas or reduce the moles of gas, so their ΔS is negative.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q45b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4B',q:'Real gases deviate most from ideal-gas behavior under which conditions?',choices:['High pressure and low temperature','Low pressure and high temperature','Low pressure and low temperature','High pressure and high temperature'],ans:0,exp:'At high pressure the molecules\' own **volume** becomes a significant fraction of the container, and at low temperature **intermolecular attractions** matter because molecules move slowly. The ideal-gas model assumes both are negligible.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q46'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4B',q:'In the van der Waals equation, the constant b corrects for:',choices:['attractions between gas molecules','elastic collisions with the walls','the finite volume of gas molecules','differences in molar mass'],ans:2,exp:'The **b term** subtracts the volume occupied by the molecules themselves (V − nb), while the **a term** corrects pressure for intermolecular attractions. Larger, more polar gases have larger a and b values.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q46b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Which weak acid and its conjugate base would make the best buffer at pH 7.4?',choices:['Acetic acid (pKa 4.8)','HF (pKa 3.2)','NH₄⁺ (pKa 9.2)','H₂PO₄⁻ (pKa 7.2)'],ans:3,exp:'A buffer works best when **pH ≈ pKa**, where acid and conjugate base are present in similar amounts; H₂PO₄⁻/HPO₄²⁻ is the closest. This is why phosphate is an important intracellular buffer.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q47'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'A buffer contains equal concentrations of a weak acid (Ka = 1×10⁻⁵) and its conjugate base. What is its pH?',choices:['2.5','5','7','9'],ans:1,exp:'With [A⁻] = [HA], the log term in **Henderson–Hasselbalch** is zero, so pH = pKa = −log(1×10⁻⁵) = 5. A value of 9 would be the pKb of the conjugate base.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q47b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Dissolving ammonium nitrate in water gives a solution that is:',choices:['slightly basic, because NO₃⁻ accepts H⁺ from water','neutral, because both ions are spectators','slightly acidic, because NH₄⁺ donates H⁺ to water','strongly acidic, because HNO₃ re-forms'],ans:2,exp:'NH₄NO₃ dissociates into NH₄⁺ and NO₃⁻. **NH₄⁺** is the conjugate acid of the weak base NH₃ and donates H⁺ (NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺), while NO₃⁻, the conjugate base of a strong acid, has negligible basicity.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q48'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'NH₃ has Kb = 1.8×10⁻⁵ at 25 °C. What is Ka for NH₄⁺?',choices:['5.6×10⁻¹⁰','1.8×10⁻⁵','1.8×10⁻⁹','5.6×10⁻⁵'],ans:0,exp:'For a conjugate pair, **Ka × Kb = Kw** = 1.0×10⁻¹⁴, so Ka = 1.0×10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 1.8×10⁻⁵ ≈ 5.6×10⁻¹⁰ (pKa ≈ 9.25). The weaker the base, the stronger its conjugate acid.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q48b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'A small amount of NaOH is added to an acetic acid/acetate buffer. The pH:',choices:['rises sharply','stays exactly constant','falls slightly','rises slightly'],ans:3,exp:'Added OH⁻ is consumed by the **weak acid** (HA + OH⁻ → A⁻ + H₂O), which only slightly raises the [A⁻]/[HA] ratio, so pH increases a little, often by less than 0.1 unit. No buffer holds pH perfectly constant.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q49'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'A buffer has its greatest capacity to absorb added acid or base when:',choices:['[HA] and [A⁻] are both high and roughly equal','[HA] is high and [A⁻] is very low','[HA] and [A⁻] are both very low','[A⁻] is high and [HA] is very low'],ans:0,exp:'**Buffer capacity** grows with the total concentration of the pair and is greatest at a 1:1 ratio (pH = pKa), since then both added H⁺ and added OH⁻ can be neutralized. A lopsided ratio leaves little reserve on one side.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q49b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Which salt forms a basic aqueous solution?',choices:['NaNO₃','NaNO₂','NH₄Cl','KCl'],ans:1,exp:'NO₂⁻ is the conjugate base of the weak acid HNO₂ and **hydrolyzes**: NO₂⁻ + H₂O ⇌ HNO₂ + OH⁻. NO₃⁻ and Cl⁻ come from strong acids and do not react, and NH₄Cl is acidic.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q50'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'A weak acid is titrated with NaOH and the equivalence point occurs near pH 8.7. Which indicator is the best choice?',choices:['Methyl orange (range 3.1–4.4)','Bromocresol green (range 3.8–5.4)','Phenolphthalein (range 8.2–10.0)','Methyl red (range 4.4–6.2)'],ans:2,exp:'An indicator should change color within a range that **brackets the equivalence-point pH**. Phenolphthalein suits weak acid–strong base titrations; methyl orange or methyl red suit strong acid–weak base titrations, whose equivalence points are acidic.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q50b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'The interconversion of a ketone and its enol, in which a hydrogen moves from the α-carbon to the oxygen, is called:',choices:['resonance','racemization','epimerization','tautomerism'],ans:3,exp:'**Tautomers** are constitutional isomers that interconvert by moving a proton and shifting a double bond. Resonance structures, by contrast, differ only in electron placement; no atoms move.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q51'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'For simple ketones such as acetone, the keto form is strongly favored over the enol form mainly because:',choices:['the C=O bond is stronger than the C=C bond','the enol form is aromatic','the enol form cannot hydrogen-bond','the keto form has more hydrogens'],ans:0,exp:'Keto and enol forms have the same atoms, but the keto form gains a stronger **C=O π bond** in place of a C=C, making it more stable. The enol fraction rises when it is stabilized by conjugation or intramolecular hydrogen bonding (as in 1,3-diketones).',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q51b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'Glycine melts (with decomposition) above 230 °C and is very water-soluble. The best explanation is that solid glycine:',choices:['forms covalent dimers through its amino groups','exists as a zwitterion with strong ionic attractions','contains a nonpolar side chain that packs tightly','is a network covalent solid like quartz'],ans:1,exp:'In the solid and near neutral pH glycine is **⁺H₃N–CH₂–COO⁻**, so its crystal is held by ionic attractions, much like a salt, and the large dipole interacts strongly with water. Its side chain is only a hydrogen atom.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q52'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'Glycine has pKa values of 2.3 (COOH) and 9.6 (NH₃⁺). What is its isoelectric point?',choices:['2.3','7.0','6.0','9.6'],ans:2,exp:'For an amino acid without an ionizable side chain, **pI = (pKa₁ + pKa₂) ÷ 2** = (2.3 + 9.6) ÷ 2 ≈ 6.0. At this pH the zwitterion predominates and the net charge is zero.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q52b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'The α and β anomers of D-glucopyranose differ in configuration only at:',choices:['C-1','C-2','C-4','C-5'],ans:0,exp:'**Anomers** differ at the anomeric carbon, C-1 of an aldose, formed when the ring closes; α has the anomeric OH trans to CH₂OH (down in a Haworth projection) and β has it cis (up). Sugars differing at C-2 or C-4 are epimers, such as mannose and galactose.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q53'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'Mutarotation of a sugar in water refers to:',choices:['oxidation of the aldehyde group to a carboxylic acid','conversion of a D-sugar into an L-sugar','isomerization of an aldose into a ketose','interconversion of α and β anomers through the open-chain form'],ans:3,exp:'The ring reopens to the **open-chain** aldehyde and recloses with either anomeric configuration, so the optical rotation drifts until an α/β equilibrium is reached. D and L configurations do not interconvert this way.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q53b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'CaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂. What volume of CO₂ at STP forms from 0.25 mol CaCO₃ with excess HCl?',choices:['2.8 L','11.2 L','5.6 L','22.4 L'],ans:2,exp:'CaCO₃ and CO₂ are 1:1, so 0.25 mol CO₂ forms; at STP 1 mol of ideal gas occupies **22.4 L**, giving 0.25 × 22.4 = 5.6 L. Applying the 2:1 HCl ratio here would wrongly give 11.2 L.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q54'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Na₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2NaCl + H₂O + CO₂. What volume of 1.0 M HCl reacts completely with 0.10 mol Na₂CO₃?',choices:['50 mL','100 mL','400 mL','200 mL'],ans:3,exp:'The ratio HCl : Na₂CO₃ is 2 : 1, so 0.20 mol HCl is needed; V = n ÷ M = 0.20 mol ÷ 1.0 M = 0.20 L = **200 mL**. Ignoring the coefficient gives 100 mL.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q54b'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry & Redox',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'In the reaction 2Fe³⁺ + Sn²⁺ → 2Fe²⁺ + Sn⁴⁺, which species is the oxidizing agent?',choices:['Sn²⁺','Fe³⁺','Fe²⁺','Sn⁴⁺'],ans:1,exp:'Fe³⁺ gains an electron (+3 → +2), so it is **reduced** and serves as the oxidizing agent. Sn²⁺ loses two electrons (+2 → +4) and is the reducing agent; products are not called agents.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q55'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry & Redox',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'What is the oxidation state of oxygen in hydrogen peroxide, H₂O₂?',choices:['−2','0','+1','−1'],ans:3,exp:'Each H is +1, so the two O atoms total −2, or **−1 each**, because the O–O bond is shared equally. Peroxide oxygen can therefore be oxidized to O₂ (0) or reduced to H₂O (−2), letting H₂O₂ act as either agent.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q55b'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'In an iodine clock reaction, which change would NOT alter the time until the blue color appears?',choices:['Adding extra starch indicator','Adding more iodide','Adding more thiosulfate','Adding more persulfate'],ans:0,exp:'Starch only **signals** free I₂ once thiosulfate is used up; it is not part of the rate-determining chemistry. Changing iodide or persulfate changes the rate of I₂ production, and changing thiosulfate changes how much I₂ must form before color appears.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q56'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'In an iodine clock reaction, the role of thiosulfate (S₂O₃²⁻) is to:',choices:['oxidize iodide to iodine','form the blue complex with iodine','immediately reduce iodine back to iodide','catalyze the slow oxidation step'],ans:2,exp:'Thiosulfate rapidly **scavenges I₂** (I₂ + 2S₂O₃²⁻ → 2I⁻ + S₄O₆²⁻), so no free iodine accumulates until thiosulfate is exhausted. The time delay therefore measures how fast the slow step makes a known amount of I₂.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q56b'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'A substance added in a trace amount speeds a reaction and is recovered unchanged at the end. It is best described as:',choices:['an intermediate','a catalyst','an inhibitor','a reactant'],ans:1,exp:'A **catalyst** provides a lower-activation-energy pathway and is regenerated, so it is not consumed overall. An intermediate is formed in one step and consumed in a later step, never present at the start.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q57'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'Dissolved Cu²⁺ ions that speed a reaction between other ions in aqueous solution are an example of:',choices:['heterogeneous catalysis','enzyme catalysis','autocatalysis','homogeneous catalysis'],ans:3,exp:'In **homogeneous catalysis** the catalyst is in the same phase as the reactants. A solid surface catalyzing gas or solution reactions (such as Pt in a catalytic converter) is heterogeneous, and autocatalysis means a product speeds its own formation.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q57b'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'Raising the temperature at constant concentrations increases reaction rate mainly because:',choices:['more collisions exceed the activation energy','the activation energy itself decreases','the reactant concentrations increase','the reaction becomes more exothermic'],ans:0,exp:'Higher temperature raises the **average kinetic energy**, shifting the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution so many more molecules exceed Ea; collision frequency also rises slightly. Ea itself is unchanged unless a catalyst is added.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q58'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'According to the Arrhenius equation, k = A·e^(−Ea/RT), the rate constant increases when:',choices:['temperature decreases or Ea increases','temperature increases or Ea increases','temperature increases or Ea decreases','temperature decreases or Ea decreases'],ans:2,exp:'A higher T or lower Ea makes the exponent **less negative**, increasing k. This is why heating and catalysis both speed reactions, and why k depends exponentially rather than linearly on temperature.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q58b'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'In an iodine clock, tetrathionate (S₄O₆²⁻) forms in the fast step as thiosulfate scavenges iodine. How does its amount change over time?',choices:['It stays near zero until the color change, then rises sharply','It rises steadily until thiosulfate is used up, then levels off','It falls steadily as iodine accumulates','It rises exponentially throughout the reaction'],ans:1,exp:'Tetrathionate forms at the rate the **slow step** produces I₂, so it builds steadily; once thiosulfate is exhausted (at the color change) no more forms and the curve plateaus. Free I₂, by contrast, stays near zero until that point and then rises.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q59'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'The experimentally observed rate law of a multistep reaction is determined primarily by:',choices:['the balanced overall equation','the fastest elementary step','the final step of the mechanism','the slowest elementary step'],ans:3,exp:'The **rate-determining (slowest) step** acts as a bottleneck, so the rate law reflects its molecularity (and any fast pre-equilibrium before it). Coefficients of the overall equation do not reliably give reaction orders.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q59b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'X and Y react according to X + 2Y → XY₂. If 3 mol X is mixed with 8 mol Y and the reaction goes to completion, what remains unreacted?',choices:['2 mol of Y','1 mol of X','5 mol of Y','No reactant remains'],ans:0,exp:'3 mol X needs 2 × 3 = 6 mol Y, so X is the **limiting reagent** and 8 − 6 = 2 mol Y is left as **excess reagent**. Subtracting moles without applying the 1:2 ratio gives the tempting 5 mol answer.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q60'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'In a chemical reaction, the limiting reagent is the reactant that:',choices:['is present in the smallest mass','is consumed first and caps the product yield','has the smallest stoichiometric coefficient','remains after the reaction is complete'],ans:1,exp:'The **limiting reagent** runs out first, so it sets the maximum (theoretical) yield. It is found by comparing mole ratios to the balanced equation, not by comparing masses or coefficients alone; the reactant left over is the excess reagent.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q60b'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'Why does raising the temperature usually increase the rate of a reaction?',choices:['The activation energy of the reaction decreases','The equilibrium constant always increases','A larger fraction of collisions have energy at least equal to Eₐ','The reaction becomes more exothermic'],ans:2,exp:'Higher temperature shifts the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution so that **more molecules have energy ≥ Eₐ**, and collisions also become more frequent. The activation energy itself is unchanged; only a catalyst lowers it.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q61'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'According to the Arrhenius equation, k = Ae^(−Eₐ/RT), which change increases the rate constant k?',choices:['Increasing the activation energy','Decreasing the temperature','Decreasing the frequency factor','Decreasing the activation energy'],ans:3,exp:'A smaller Eₐ makes the exponent −Eₐ/RT less negative, so **k increases**; raising T or the frequency factor A also increases k. Raising Eₐ or lowering T makes the exponential term smaller and slows the reaction.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q61b'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'Which description fits the reaction-coordinate energy diagram of an exothermic reaction?',choices:['Products lie above reactants, with an energy peak between them','Products lie below reactants, with no energy peak between them','Products and reactants lie at the same energy, with a peak','Products lie below reactants, with an energy peak between them'],ans:3,exp:'In an **exothermic** reaction the products are lower in energy than the reactants (ΔH < 0), but the reactants must still climb over a transition-state hump (**Eₐ > 0**). Products above reactants describes an endothermic reaction.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q62'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'A reaction has a forward activation energy of 50 kJ/mol and ΔH = −30 kJ/mol. What is the activation energy of the reverse reaction?',choices:['20 kJ/mol','80 kJ/mol','50 kJ/mol','30 kJ/mol'],ans:1,exp:'For the reverse reaction, products must climb back to the same transition state, which sits 50 kJ/mol above the reactants and 30 kJ/mol further above the products: **Eₐ(reverse) = Eₐ(forward) − ΔH = 50 + 30 = 80 kJ/mol**. For an exothermic reaction the reverse barrier is always larger.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q62b'},
  {s:'Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'A molecule has the formula AB₂, with A as the central atom. What additional information is needed to predict whether it is linear or bent?',choices:['The molar mass of the molecule','The number of lone pairs on the central atom','The number of neutrons in atom A','The electronegativity of the B atoms'],ans:1,exp:'VSEPR geometry depends on the number of **electron domains** around the central atom, including lone pairs. CO₂ (no lone pairs on C) is linear, while H₂O (two lone pairs on O) is bent, even though both are triatomic.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q63'},
  {s:'Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'What is the molecular geometry of SO₂?',choices:['Linear','Trigonal planar','Bent','Trigonal pyramidal'],ans:2,exp:'Sulfur in SO₂ has three electron domains (two bonding regions and **one lone pair**), giving a trigonal planar electron geometry and a **bent** molecular shape (~119°). Assuming linearity by analogy to CO₂ ignores the lone pair.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q63b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'A triacylglycerol has three different fatty acids esterified to the three glycerol hydroxyl groups. How many stereocenters does the glycerol backbone contain?',choices:['0','1','2','3'],ans:1,exp:'Glycerol C-2 bears H, an ester oxygen and two CH₂O–acyl groups; when the acyl groups on C-1 and C-3 differ, those two substituents are different and **C-2 becomes a stereocenter**. C-1 and C-3 each carry two hydrogens, so they can never be chiral.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q64'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'Tristearin is a triacylglycerol with the same saturated fatty acid at all three positions. Which statement about its chirality is correct?',choices:['It is chiral, with one stereocenter at C-2','It is chiral, with stereocenters at C-1 and C-3','It is chiral, with three stereocenters','It is achiral, because C-2 has two identical groups'],ans:3,exp:'With identical acyl groups at C-1 and C-3, glycerol C-2 carries **two identical CH₂O–acyl substituents**, so it is not a stereocenter and the molecule is achiral. A stereocenter requires four different groups.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q64b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'Chemically, a typical soap is best described as:',choices:['the sodium salt of a long-chain carboxylic acid','the sodium salt of a long-chain sulfonic acid','an ester of glycerol and a long-chain acid','a long-chain primary alcohol'],ans:0,exp:'Soap is a **fatty acid carboxylate salt**, R–COO⁻ Na⁺, made by saponification of fats. Sulfonate salts are synthetic detergents, and glycerol esters are the fats themselves.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q65'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'Heating a triacylglycerol with aqueous NaOH (saponification) gives which products?',choices:['Glycerol and three free fatty acids','Glycerol and three fatty acid methyl esters','Glycerol and three fatty acid sodium salts','A diacylglycerol and one fatty acid salt'],ans:2,exp:'Base hydrolysis cleaves all three ester bonds, giving **glycerol and three carboxylate (soap) salts**. Free fatty acids form only on acidic workup or acid-catalyzed hydrolysis, and methyl esters come from transesterification with methanol.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q65b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'Saponification of a pure triacylglycerol containing cis-unsaturated chains gives one more distinct carboxylate salt than expected. What is the most likely explanation?',choices:['Some unsaturated chains isomerized, for example cis to trans','The saturated chain was cleaved into two shorter chains','The glycerol was converted into an extra carboxylate salt','The ester bonds were reduced to ether linkages'],ans:0,exp:'Under hot basic conditions a C=C bond can change geometry or position, and a **cis/trans or positional isomer** is a distinct compound, so it shows up as an extra salt. Saponification does not break C–C bonds, and glycerol is released as an alcohol, not a salt.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q66'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'Compared with its cis isomer, a trans-unsaturated fatty acid of the same chain length has:',choices:['a lower melting point, because its chain is kinked','a higher melting point, because its chains pack more tightly','the same melting point, because the formulas are identical','a lower melting point, because it forms weaker ionic bonds'],ans:1,exp:'A **trans** double bond keeps the chain nearly straight, so molecules pack closely and London dispersion forces are stronger, which **raises the melting point**. The cis double bond introduces a kink that disrupts packing, which is why cis oils are liquid.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q66b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'How many moles of NaOH are consumed in the complete saponification of 2 mol of a triacylglycerol?',choices:['2 mol','3 mol','6 mol','1 mol'],ans:2,exp:'Each triacylglycerol has **three ester groups**, and each consumes one OH⁻, so 2 mol × 3 = **6 mol NaOH**. The base is a reactant, not a catalyst, in this reaction.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q67'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'Why is hydroxide consumed, rather than acting as a catalyst, in base-promoted ester hydrolysis?',choices:['Hydroxide is permanently bonded to the glycerol','Hydroxide is oxidized to O₂ during the reaction','Na⁺ is reduced to sodium metal during the reaction','The carboxylic acid formed is deprotonated to carboxylate'],ans:3,exp:'The carboxylic acid released is far more acidic than water or the alkoxide, so it is immediately **deprotonated to a resonance-stabilized carboxylate**, using up one equivalent of base. This irreversible step also drives the reaction to completion.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q67b'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'When soap molecules form a micelle in water, how are they arranged?',choices:['Ionic heads point inward and hydrocarbon tails face the water','Hydrocarbon tails point inward and ionic heads face the water','Molecules form a flat bilayer with heads facing each other','Molecules dissolve individually with no organized structure'],ans:1,exp:'Soaps are **amphipathic**: nonpolar tails cluster in the micelle core away from water, while charged carboxylate heads face the aqueous solvent. Grease dissolves in the nonpolar core, which is how soap cleans.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q68'},
  {s:'Organic Chemistry & Lab Techniques',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'Which molecule is amphipathic?',choices:['Tristearin','Cholesteryl stearate','Phosphatidylcholine','Octane'],ans:2,exp:'**Phosphatidylcholine** has a charged phosphate–choline head and two nonpolar fatty acyl tails, so it has both hydrophilic and hydrophobic regions and forms bilayers. Tristearin and cholesteryl esters are almost entirely nonpolar storage lipids.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q68b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'A compound is 40.0% C, 6.7% H and 53.3% O by mass. What is its empirical formula?',choices:['CHO','CH₂O₂','C₂H₄O','CH₂O'],ans:3,exp:'In 100 g: 40.0/12.0 = 3.33 mol C, 6.7/1.0 = 6.7 mol H and 53.3/16.0 = 3.33 mol O; dividing by 3.33 gives a **1:2:1** ratio, so the empirical formula is **CH₂O**. Always convert mass to moles before finding the ratio.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q69'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Which pair of compounds has the same empirical formula?',choices:['Ethane (C₂H₆) and ethene (C₂H₄)','Glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆) and acetic acid (C₂H₄O₂)','Benzene (C₆H₆) and toluene (C₇H₈)','Methanol (CH₄O) and ethanol (C₂H₆O)'],ans:1,exp:'Glucose and acetic acid both reduce to **CH₂O**, the simplest whole-number ratio. Methanol and ethanol are homologs whose formulas differ by CH₂, so their empirical formulas (CH₄O and C₂H₆O) differ.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q69b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'50 mL of 0.30 M NaCl is mixed with 50 mL of 0.10 M Na₂SO₄. What is [Na⁺] in the mixture?',choices:['0.20 M','0.50 M','0.25 M','0.35 M'],ans:2,exp:'Na⁺ from NaCl: 0.050 L × 0.30 M = 15 mmol; from Na₂SO₄: 0.050 L × 0.10 M × **2** = 10 mmol. Total 25 mmol in 100 mL gives **0.25 M**. Forgetting the 2 Na⁺ per formula unit gives 0.20 M, and forgetting the dilution to 100 mL gives 0.50 M.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q70'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'25 mL of 2.0 M HCl is diluted with water to a final volume of 100 mL. What is the new concentration?',choices:['0.50 M','0.25 M','1.0 M','8.0 M'],ans:0,exp:'Using **M₁V₁ = M₂V₂**: (2.0 M)(25 mL) = M₂(100 mL), so M₂ = **0.50 M**. Dilution keeps moles of solute constant while volume increases.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q70b'},
  {s:'Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'Which compound can form hydrogen bonds with water?',choices:['Propane','Ethene','Methylamine','Hexane'],ans:2,exp:'**Methylamine** has an N–H bond (an H donor) and a lone pair on N (an H acceptor), so it hydrogen-bonds with water. Hydrocarbons have only C–H bonds, which are not polar enough to donate hydrogen bonds and have no lone pairs to accept them.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q71'},
  {s:'Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'Which compound can accept hydrogen bonds from water but cannot donate them?',choices:['Diethyl ether','Ethanol','Ethylamine','Acetic acid'],ans:0,exp:'**Diethyl ether** has lone pairs on oxygen to accept H-bonds but no O–H or N–H bond to donate one. Ethanol, ethylamine and acetic acid all have H bonded to O or N, so they can both donate and accept.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q71b'},
  {s:'Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'Which chloromethane has no net dipole moment?',choices:['CH₃Cl','CH₂Cl₂','CHCl₃','CCl₄'],ans:3,exp:'In **CCl₄** four identical C–Cl bond dipoles point to the corners of a regular tetrahedron and cancel, giving zero net dipole. In the partly substituted molecules the C–H and C–Cl dipoles differ, so they do not cancel.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q72'},
  {s:'Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'Which molecule has polar bonds but is nonpolar overall?',choices:['H₂O','CO₂','NH₃','SO₂'],ans:1,exp:'**CO₂** is linear, so its two equal C=O bond dipoles point in opposite directions and cancel. H₂O and SO₂ are bent and NH₃ is trigonal pyramidal, so their bond dipoles add to a net dipole.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q72b'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'What is the electron configuration of the sulfide ion, S²⁻?',choices:['1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁴','1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶','1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p²','1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s²'],ans:1,exp:'Neutral S has 16 electrons (…3p⁴); gaining two gives **18 electrons, 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶**, isoelectronic with argon. The 3p⁴ configuration is the neutral atom, not the anion.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q73'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Which ion is isoelectronic with neon?',choices:['Ca²⁺','Cl⁻','Mg²⁺','S²⁻'],ans:2,exp:'**Mg²⁺** has 12 − 2 = 10 electrons, the same as Ne. Ca²⁺, Cl⁻ and S²⁻ each have 18 electrons and are isoelectronic with argon.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q73b'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'Acetate hydrolyzes by CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O ⇌ CH₃COOH + OH⁻. If a sodium acetate solution is diluted with water, what happens to the fraction of acetate that is hydrolyzed?',choices:['It increases','It decreases','It stays the same','It drops to zero'],ans:0,exp:'Dilution lowers all solute concentrations, and because the expression has two dissolved species on the product side and one on the reactant side, **Q drops below K** and the equilibrium shifts right. More water drives a larger fraction of acetate to hydrolyze, even though [OH⁻] itself falls.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q74'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'Solid NaOH is added to a solution in which CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O ⇌ CH₃COOH + OH⁻ is at equilibrium. How does the equilibrium respond?',choices:['It shifts right, raising [CH₃COOH]','It does not shift, because Na⁺ is a spectator','It shifts right, lowering [CH₃COO⁻]','It shifts left, lowering [CH₃COOH]'],ans:3,exp:'Adding OH⁻, a **product**, makes Q > K, so by **Le Châtelier\'s principle** the reaction shifts left, consuming acetic acid and re-forming acetate. Na⁺ is indeed a spectator, but the added hydroxide is not.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q74b'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'Why is atmospheric N₂ so chemically unreactive?',choices:['Its N≡N triple bond is very strong','Its molecules are highly polar','Its atoms have incomplete octets','Its atoms share a weak single bond'],ans:0,exp:'The **N≡N triple bond** (about 945 kJ/mol) is one of the strongest bonds known, so breaking it requires a large energy input. N₂ is nonpolar and each N has a complete octet; only specialized enzymes (nitrogenase) or industrial catalysts break it readily.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q75'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry & Redox',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Nitrogen-fixing bacteria convert N₂ into NH₃. What happens to the oxidation state of nitrogen?',choices:['It goes from 0 to +3, an oxidation','It goes from 0 to −3, a reduction','It goes from 0 to +5, an oxidation','It goes from +3 to −3, a reduction'],ans:1,exp:'N in elemental N₂ is **0**; in NH₃ each H is +1, so N is **−3**. A decrease in oxidation number is a **reduction**, which is why nitrogenase requires electrons (and ATP).',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q75b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'What is the conjugate acid of HPO₄²⁻?',choices:['PO₄³⁻','H₃PO₄','H₂PO₄⁻','H₂P₂O₇²⁻'],ans:2,exp:'A conjugate acid has **one more H⁺** than its base: HPO₄²⁻ + H⁺ → **H₂PO₄⁻**, and the charge rises by one. PO₄³⁻ is its conjugate base, and H₃PO₄ differs by two protons.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q76'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Which species is amphiprotic (able to act as both a Brønsted acid and a Brønsted base)?',choices:['CO₃²⁻','NH₄⁺','Cl⁻','HCO₃⁻'],ans:3,exp:'**HCO₃⁻** can donate its H⁺ (forming CO₃²⁻) or accept one (forming H₂CO₃), so it is amphiprotic. CO₃²⁻ has no H⁺ to donate, NH₄⁺ cannot accept another proton, and Cl⁻ is a negligibly weak base.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q76b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'For CH₃COOH(aq) + H₂O(l) ⇌ H₃O⁺(aq) + CH₃COO⁻(aq), which is the correct Kₐ expression?',choices:['[H₃O⁺][CH₃COO⁻] / ([CH₃COOH][H₂O])','[H₃O⁺][CH₃COO⁻] / [CH₃COOH]','[CH₃COOH] / ([H₃O⁺][CH₃COO⁻])','[H₃O⁺][CH₃COO⁻][H₂O] / [CH₃COOH]'],ans:1,exp:'Pure liquids and the solvent are left out of equilibrium expressions because their concentration (activity) is **essentially constant** in dilute solution, so Kₐ = [H₃O⁺][CH₃COO⁻] / [CH₃COOH]. Including [H₂O] is the classic error.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q77'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Powdered limestone (CaCO₃) is added to acidic soil. What is the main effect?',choices:['It raises pH, because carbonate consumes H⁺','It lowers pH, because Ca²⁺ is a strong acid','It lowers pH, because CO₂ gas is released','It has no effect, because CaCO₃ is insoluble'],ans:0,exp:'Carbonate is a base: **CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → H₂O + CO₂**, so liming neutralizes soil acid and raises pH. Although CaCO₃ is poorly soluble in water, it dissolves in acid, which is exactly the condition where it is needed.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q77b'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry & Redox',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4C',q:'In any electrochemical cell, galvanic or electrolytic, what happens at the cathode?',choices:['Oxidation occurs','Electrons are lost by the reactant','Reduction occurs','The electrode always dissolves'],ans:2,exp:'The **cathode is always the site of reduction** (gain of electrons), and the anode is always the site of oxidation: "An Ox, Red Cat." Only the sign of the electrodes changes between galvanic and electrolytic cells.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q78'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry & Redox',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4C',q:'In a galvanic cell, how do electrons travel?',choices:['From cathode to anode through the wire','From anode to cathode through the wire','From anode to cathode through the salt bridge','From cathode to anode through the salt bridge'],ans:1,exp:'Electrons are released by oxidation at the **anode** and flow through the **external wire** to the cathode, where reduction uses them. The salt bridge carries ions, not electrons, to keep each half-cell electrically neutral.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q78b'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'The Ca²⁺ ion has the same electron configuration as which noble gas?',choices:['Neon','Krypton','Xenon','Argon'],ans:3,exp:'Calcium (Z = 20) loses its two 4s electrons to form Ca²⁺, leaving **18 electrons: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶**, the argon configuration. Group 2 metals form 2+ ions to reach the preceding noble gas configuration.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q79'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'What is the ground-state electron configuration of Fe²⁺?',choices:['[Ar] 4s² 3d⁴','[Ar] 3d⁶','[Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵','[Ar] 3d⁴'],ans:1,exp:'Neutral Fe is [Ar] 4s² 3d⁶, and transition metals lose their **4s electrons first** when they ionize, so Fe²⁺ is **[Ar] 3d⁶**. Removing the 3d electrons first, giving 4s² 3d⁴, is the common mistake.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q79b'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Which alkali metal reacts most vigorously with water?',choices:['Lithium','Sodium','Rubidium','Potassium'],ans:2,exp:'Reactivity of metals with water **increases down a group** because the valence electron is farther from the nucleus and better shielded, so the **ionization energy is lower**. Among these, rubidium is lowest in the group and loses its electron most easily.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q80'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Moving down group 2 of the periodic table, which property increases?',choices:['Atomic radius','First ionization energy','Electronegativity','Second ionization energy'],ans:0,exp:'Each step down adds an electron shell, so **atomic radius increases**. Because the valence electrons are then farther from the nucleus, ionization energies and electronegativity all decrease.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q80b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4B',q:'Calcium reacts with water: Ca + 2H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ + H₂. What volume of H₂ is produced from 0.80 g Ca at 300 K and 1.0 atm? (Ca = 40 g/mol; R = 0.0821 L·atm/(mol·K))',choices:['0.25 L','0.45 L','0.98 L','0.49 L'],ans:3,exp:'0.80 g ÷ 40 g/mol = 0.020 mol Ca, giving **0.020 mol H₂** (1:1). V = nRT/P = 0.020 × 0.0821 × 300 / 1.0 ≈ **0.49 L**; using 22.4 L/mol gives 0.45 L, which applies only at 273 K.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q81'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry & Redox',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Which mixture reacts spontaneously?',choices:['Br₂ added to aqueous NaCl','I₂ added to aqueous NaBr','Cl₂ added to aqueous NaBr','I₂ added to aqueous NaCl'],ans:2,exp:'Oxidizing strength of the halogens decreases down the group (F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂), so a halogen **oxidizes the halide ions below it**: Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂. A weaker oxidant such as Br₂ or I₂ cannot oxidize a halide above it.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q81b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Ethene (C₂H₄) and cyclohexane (C₆H₁₂) necessarily have the same:',choices:['molecular formula','empirical formula','molar mass','boiling point'],ans:1,exp:'Both reduce to the simplest ratio **CH₂**, so their empirical formulas match while their molecular formulas, molar masses and properties differ. In general, compounds made of whole-number multiples of the same unit, such as a dimer and a trimer, share an empirical formula.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q82'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'A hydrocarbon has the empirical formula CH and a molar mass of 78 g/mol. What is its molecular formula?',choices:['C₆H₆','C₂H₂','C₄H₄','C₈H₈'],ans:0,exp:'The CH unit has a mass of 13 g/mol, and 78 ÷ 13 = **6**, so the molecular formula is **C₆H₆** (benzene). Molecular formula = empirical formula × (molar mass ÷ empirical-formula mass).',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q82b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4B',q:'For N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, 2.0 mol N₂ is mixed with 4.5 mol H₂. What is the maximum amount of NH₃ that can form?',choices:['1.5 mol','4.0 mol','4.5 mol','3.0 mol'],ans:3,exp:'2.0 mol N₂ would need 6.0 mol H₂, but only 4.5 mol is present, so **H₂ is limiting**; 4.5 mol H₂ × (2 NH₃ / 3 H₂) = **3.0 mol NH₃**. Basing the yield on N₂ gives the incorrect 4.0 mol.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q83'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4B',q:'What volume does 0.25 mol of an ideal gas occupy at STP (0 °C and 1 atm)?',choices:['11.2 L','5.6 L','6.1 L','22.4 L'],ans:1,exp:'At STP one mole of ideal gas occupies **22.4 L**, so 0.25 mol × 22.4 L/mol = **5.6 L**. The 6.1 L value uses 24.5 L/mol, which is correct only at 25 °C.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q83b'},
  {s:'Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'Which description of phosphine, PH₃, is correct?',choices:['No lone pairs on P; trigonal planar','One lone pair on P; bent','One lone pair on P; trigonal pyramidal','Two lone pairs on P; trigonal pyramidal'],ans:2,exp:'P has 5 valence electrons: three form P–H bonds and two form **one lone pair**, giving four electron domains and a **trigonal pyramidal** shape like NH₃. Trigonal planar would require no lone pair, as in BF₃.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q84'},
  {s:'Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'Which ranks the H–X–H bond angles correctly from largest to smallest?',choices:['H₂O > NH₃ > CH₄','NH₃ > CH₄ > H₂O','CH₄ > H₂O > NH₃','CH₄ > NH₃ > H₂O'],ans:3,exp:'All three have four electron domains, but **lone pairs repel more strongly than bonding pairs** and squeeze the bond angles: CH₄ 109.5° (no lone pairs) > NH₃ ≈ 107° (one) > H₂O ≈ 104.5° (two).',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q84b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4B',q:'Equal masses of helium and argon are held at the same temperature and pressure. Which gas occupies the larger volume?',choices:['Argon, because each atom has more mass','Helium, because it contains more moles','Neither, because the masses are equal','Argon, because it contains more moles'],ans:1,exp:'Helium has a much smaller molar mass (4 vs 40 g/mol), so the same mass contains about **10 times as many moles**. At fixed T and P, **V = nRT/P** is proportional to moles, so helium takes up more volume at every temperature.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q85'},
  {s:'Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'Which carbon–carbon bond is the shortest?',choices:['The C–C bond in ethane','The C=C bond in ethene','The C≡C bond in ethyne','A C–C bond in benzene'],ans:2,exp:'**Higher bond order means a shorter, stronger bond**: the triple bond in ethyne (~1.20 Å) is shorter than the double bond in ethene (~1.34 Å). Benzene bonds have an order of 1.5 (~1.40 Å), between single and double.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q85b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'The reaction Zn(s) + CuSO₄(aq) → ZnSO₄(aq) + Cu(s) is best classified as:',choices:['a single-replacement reaction','a combination reaction','a double-replacement reaction','a decomposition reaction'],ans:0,exp:'In a **single-replacement** reaction one element displaces another from a compound; here Zn replaces Cu²⁺. In a double replacement, two compounds swap partners, and no element appears alone on either side.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q86'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'The reaction AgNO₃(aq) + NaCl(aq) → AgCl(s) + NaNO₃(aq) is best classified as:',choices:['a single-replacement reaction','a combination reaction','a decomposition reaction','a double-replacement reaction'],ans:3,exp:'Two ionic compounds exchange partners (Ag⁺ pairs with Cl⁻ and Na⁺ with NO₃⁻), which is a **double-replacement (metathesis)** reaction; here it is driven by precipitation of insoluble AgCl. No element is displaced by a free element, so it is not single replacement.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q86b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Bacteria reduce sulfate so that each SO₄²⁻ ion yields one H₂S molecule. What mass of H₂S forms from 0.50 mol of sulfate? (H₂S = 34 g/mol)',choices:['8.5 g','34 g','17 g','48 g'],ans:2,exp:'The 1:1 ratio gives 0.50 mol H₂S, and 0.50 mol × 34 g/mol = **17 g**. The 48 g value is the mass of the sulfate itself (96 g/mol × 0.50), not of the product.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q87'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'For 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, what mass of water forms when 4.0 g of H₂ reacts completely with excess O₂?',choices:['18 g','36 g','72 g','9.0 g'],ans:1,exp:'4.0 g H₂ ÷ 2.0 g/mol = 2.0 mol H₂, which gives **2.0 mol H₂O** (2:2 ratio); 2.0 mol × 18 g/mol = **36 g**. Always convert grams to moles before applying the mole ratio.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q87b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Which Kₐ value corresponds to a weak acid?',choices:['1.0 × 10³','5.0 × 10⁷','1.8 × 10⁻⁵','1.0 × 10²'],ans:2,exp:'A **weak acid** ionizes only slightly, so its Kₐ is much less than 1 (here 1.8 × 10⁻⁵, the value for acetic acid). Strong acids are essentially fully ionized and have Kₐ ≫ 1.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q88'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Acid X has pKₐ = 4.2 and acid Y has pKₐ = 9.3. Which statement is correct?',choices:['X is stronger and has the larger Kₐ','X is stronger and has the smaller Kₐ','Y is stronger and has the larger Kₐ','Y is stronger and has the smaller Kₐ'],ans:0,exp:'Because **pKₐ = −log Kₐ**, a lower pKₐ means a larger Kₐ and a stronger acid, so X is stronger by about five orders of magnitude. A larger pKₐ number corresponds to a weaker acid.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q88b'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry & Redox',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'In the reaction Zn(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) → Zn²⁺(aq) + Cu(s), which species is the reducing agent?',choices:['Cu²⁺','Zn','Zn²⁺','Cu'],ans:1,exp:'The **reducing agent is the species that is oxidized**: Zn goes from 0 to +2, giving its electrons to Cu²⁺. Cu²⁺ is reduced and is therefore the oxidizing agent; Zn²⁺ and Cu are products.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q89'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'What happens to the pH of water as more CO₂ dissolves in it?',choices:['It rises, because carbonate ions form','It is unchanged, because CO₂ is nonpolar','It rises, because H₃O⁺ is consumed','It falls, because carbonic acid forms'],ans:3,exp:'CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂CO₃ ⇌ **H⁺ + HCO₃⁻**, so dissolved CO₂ acts as a weak acid and lowers pH. The same equilibrium links blood CO₂ to pH, which is why hypoventilation causes respiratory acidosis.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q89b'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'On a phase diagram, a substance is heated at constant pressure so that its state crosses the liquid–vapor boundary. Which process occurs?',choices:['Condensation','Sublimation','Vaporization','Melting'],ans:2,exp:'Crossing the liquid–vapor curve toward higher temperature converts liquid to gas, which is **vaporization**. Crossing the same line in the other direction (cooling) is condensation; sublimation crosses the solid–vapor line.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q90'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'Above both its critical temperature and critical pressure, a substance exists as:',choices:['a solid at any higher pressure','a supercritical fluid','three phases in equilibrium','a liquid that cannot vaporize'],ans:1,exp:'Beyond the **critical point** the liquid and gas phases have the same density and become indistinguishable, forming a **supercritical fluid**. Three phases coexist only at the **triple point**.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q90b'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'In the Bohr model, what happens when a hydrogen electron moves from n = 4 to n = 2?',choices:['It emits a photon whose energy equals the level difference','It absorbs a photon whose energy equals the level difference','It emits a photon whose energy equals that of the n = 4 level','It neither emits nor absorbs any energy'],ans:0,exp:'Falling to a **lower** level releases energy as a photon with **ΔE = E₄ − E₂ = hf**; jumping to a higher level requires absorbing a photon of that same energy. For n = 4 → 2 the photon is visible (the blue-green Balmer line).',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q91'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Which hydrogen-atom transition emits the photon with the shortest wavelength?',choices:['n = 3 → n = 2','n = 4 → n = 3','n = 2 → n = 1','n = 3 → n = 1'],ans:3,exp:'Energy gaps are proportional to (1/n_f² − 1/n_i²), so 3 → 1 releases the most energy (8/9 of the ionization energy, versus 3/4 for 2 → 1). Because **E = hc/λ**, the largest energy gives the **shortest wavelength**.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q91b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'A metal hydroxide M(OH)₂ has molar solubility s. Which expression gives its Kₛₚ?',choices:['s²','2s³','4s³','27s⁴'],ans:2,exp:'M(OH)₂ ⇌ M²⁺ + 2OH⁻, so [M²⁺] = s and [OH⁻] = 2s; Kₛₚ = s(2s)² = **4s³**. Forgetting to square the doubled hydroxide concentration gives 2s³.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q92'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Silver phosphate, Ag₃PO₄, has molar solubility s. Which expression gives its Kₛₚ?',choices:['s⁴','3s⁴','27s⁴','108s⁵'],ans:2,exp:'Ag₃PO₄ ⇌ 3Ag⁺ + PO₄³⁻, so [Ag⁺] = 3s and [PO₄³⁻] = s; Kₛₚ = (3s)³(s) = **27s⁴**. The coefficient becomes both a multiplier and an exponent.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q92b'},
  {s:'Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'Why is water a liquid at room temperature while the heavier hydrides H₂S and H₂Se are gases?',choices:['Water has much stronger London dispersion forces','Water molecules form ionic bonds with each other','Water molecules hydrogen-bond to one another','Water has a larger molar mass than H₂S'],ans:2,exp:'Oxygen is small and highly electronegative, so the O–H bonds let water form extensive **hydrogen bonds**, which greatly raise its boiling point. Dispersion forces actually increase with molar mass, which would favor the heavier hydrides.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q93'},
  {s:'Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'The boiling points of H₂S, H₂Se and H₂Te increase in that order mainly because of increasing:',choices:['dispersion forces','hydrogen bonding','ion–dipole attractions','covalent bond polarity'],ans:0,exp:'Larger atoms have bigger, more **polarizable** electron clouds, so **London dispersion forces** grow with molar mass and raise the boiling point. Bond polarity actually decreases from S to Te, and none of these hydrides hydrogen-bonds significantly.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q93b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4B',q:'A gas mixture contains 2.0 mol N₂ and 3.0 mol O₂ at a total pressure of 10 atm. What is the partial pressure of N₂?',choices:['2.0 atm','4.0 atm','5.0 atm','6.0 atm'],ans:1,exp:'By **Dalton\'s law**, P(N₂) = X(N₂) × P(total) = (2.0/5.0) × 10 atm = **4.0 atm**. The 6.0 atm value is the partial pressure of O₂.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q94'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4B',q:'Propane burns completely: C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O(g). What is the mole fraction of water vapor in the gaseous products?',choices:['3/7','1/2','2/3','4/7'],ans:3,exp:'The products contain 3 mol CO₂ + 4 mol H₂O = 7 mol of gas, so X(H₂O) = **4/7**. Multiplying this mole fraction by the total pressure gives the partial pressure of water.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q94b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Which silver halide is freely soluble in water?',choices:['AgCl','AgBr','AgI','AgF'],ans:3,exp:'**AgF** is the exception: strong hydration of the small F⁻ ion outweighs its lattice energy, so it is very soluble, while AgCl (white), AgBr (cream) and AgI (yellow) all precipitate. This difference lets Ag⁺ distinguish fluoride from the other halides.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q95'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Which compound is insoluble in water?',choices:['Na₂CO₃','KNO₃','CaCO₃','(NH₄)₂SO₄'],ans:2,exp:'Most carbonates are insoluble, so **CaCO₃** precipitates. Salts of the alkali metals and ammonium, and all nitrates, are soluble, which is why the other three dissolve.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q95b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Under which condition will a precipitate form when two salt solutions are mixed?',choices:['When the ion product Q exceeds Kₛₚ','When the ion product Q is less than Kₛₚ','When the ion product Q equals zero','When Kₛₚ is greater than 1'],ans:0,exp:'If **Q > Kₛₚ**, the solution is supersaturated and solid precipitates until Q falls to Kₛₚ. If Q < Kₛₚ the solution is unsaturated and more solid could dissolve; Q = Kₛₚ means saturated equilibrium.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q96'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Solutions are mixed so that [Ag⁺] = 1.0 × 10⁻⁴ M and [Cl⁻] = 1.0 × 10⁻⁴ M. Kₛₚ for AgCl is 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰. What happens?',choices:['No precipitate forms, because Q is less than Kₛₚ','AgCl precipitates, because Q is greater than Kₛₚ','No precipitate forms, because Q equals Kₛₚ','AgCl precipitates, because Q is less than Kₛₚ'],ans:1,exp:'Q = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻] = (1.0 × 10⁻⁴)² = **1.0 × 10⁻⁸**, which is larger than 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰. Because **Q > Kₛₚ**, AgCl precipitates.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q96b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'In a test panel, cation M gives a precipitate with only one of six anions, while every other cation tested precipitates with three or more. What can be concluded?',choices:['Salts of M are generally the least soluble','M must be a transition-metal ion','Salts of M are generally the most soluble','M forms no ionic compounds'],ans:2,exp:'Each "no reaction" result means that salt stayed dissolved, so the cation with the **fewest precipitates forms the most soluble salts** overall. A single precipitate still shows that M does form ionic solids, just fewer insoluble ones.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q97'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Which cation forms water-soluble salts with essentially all common anions?',choices:['Ag⁺','Pb²⁺','Ba²⁺','Na⁺'],ans:3,exp:'By the solubility rules, **alkali-metal (group 1) and ammonium salts are soluble**. Ag⁺ and Pb²⁺ form insoluble halides, and Ba²⁺ forms insoluble sulfate and carbonate.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q97b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Which 1:1 salt has the lowest molar solubility in water?',choices:['AgCl (Kₛₚ = 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰)','AgBr (Kₛₚ = 5.0 × 10⁻¹³)','BaSO₄ (Kₛₚ = 1.1 × 10⁻¹⁰)','CaSO₄ (Kₛₚ = 2.4 × 10⁻⁵)'],ans:1,exp:'For salts with the **same ion ratio**, molar solubility is √Kₛₚ, so the smallest Kₛₚ (AgBr) means the lowest solubility. The same logic explains why BaSO₄, despite Ba²⁺ being toxic, is safe as a GI contrast agent: so little dissolves that free Ba²⁺ stays very low.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q98'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Ag₂CrO₄ has Kₛₚ = 1.1 × 10⁻¹² and AgCl has Kₛₚ = 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰. Which has the greater molar solubility?',choices:['Ag₂CrO₄, because s = (Kₛₚ/4)^(1/3)','AgCl, because its Kₛₚ is larger','Neither; their molar solubilities are equal','AgCl, because chloride is the smaller anion'],ans:0,exp:'For Ag₂CrO₄, s = (1.1 × 10⁻¹²/4)^(1/3) ≈ **6.5 × 10⁻⁵ M**; for AgCl, s = √(1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰) ≈ **1.3 × 10⁻⁵ M**. Kₛₚ values can be compared directly only for salts with the **same stoichiometry**.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q98b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'A solution contains either F⁻ or Cl⁻. Adding which reagent would tell them apart?',choices:['NaNO₃','KNO₃','Ca(NO₃)₂','NH₄NO₃'],ans:2,exp:'**CaF₂ is insoluble** while CaCl₂ is soluble, so Ca²⁺ gives a precipitate only with fluoride. Sodium, potassium and ammonium salts of both halides are soluble, so those reagents show no difference.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q99'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Solid NaF is added to a saturated solution of CaF₂. What happens to the solubility of CaF₂?',choices:['It increases, because more ions are present','It is unchanged, because Kₛₚ is constant','It increases, because Na⁺ forms a complex','It decreases, because of the common-ion effect'],ans:3,exp:'Added F⁻ raises Q above Kₛₚ, shifting CaF₂ ⇌ Ca²⁺ + 2F⁻ to the left, so **less CaF₂ dissolves (common-ion effect)**. Kₛₚ itself stays constant, but the solubility at that Kₛₚ drops.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q99b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'How many moles of potassium hydrogen phthalate (KHP, 204.2 g/mol) are in a 0.4084 g sample?',choices:['2.000 × 10⁻³ mol','4.000 × 10⁻³ mol','1.000 × 10⁻³ mol','2.000 × 10⁻² mol'],ans:0,exp:'**Moles = mass ÷ molar mass** = 0.4084 g ÷ 204.2 g/mol = 2.000 × 10⁻³ mol. KHP is used as a **primary standard** because it is pure, stable and non-hygroscopic, so its mass gives an exact mole count.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q100'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Exactly 2.000 × 10⁻³ mol of KHP (a monoprotic acid) requires 20.00 mL of NaOH to reach the equivalence point. What is the NaOH concentration?',choices:['0.0500 M','0.1000 M','0.2000 M','0.0100 M'],ans:1,exp:'At equivalence, mol NaOH = mol KHP (1:1), so M = 2.000 × 10⁻³ mol ÷ 0.02000 L = **0.1000 M**. This **standardization** gives the exact titrant concentration needed for later titrations.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q100b'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'Solid NaOH dissolves readily in water, and the solution becomes noticeably warmer. Which signs apply to this dissolution?',choices:['ΔH > 0 and ΔG < 0','ΔH < 0 and ΔG > 0','ΔH < 0 and ΔG < 0','ΔH > 0 and ΔG > 0'],ans:2,exp:'Heat released to the water means the process is **exothermic (ΔH < 0)**, and dissolving on its own means it is **spontaneous (ΔG < 0)**. A favorable ΔH alone can make ΔG negative even if the entropy change is small.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q101'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'NH₄NO₃ dissolves spontaneously in water even though the solution becomes cold. What must be true?',choices:['ΔS > 0, and TΔS is larger than ΔH','ΔS < 0, and TΔS is larger than ΔH','ΔH < 0, and ΔS is close to zero','ΔG > 0, and ΔH is larger than TΔS'],ans:0,exp:'Cooling means **ΔH > 0**, so for ΔG = ΔH − TΔS to be negative, the process must be **entropy-driven**: ΔS > 0 with TΔS exceeding ΔH. This is how instant cold packs work.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q101b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'4.0 g of NaOH (40 g/mol) is dissolved in enough water to make 500 mL of solution. What is the molarity?',choices:['0.10 M','0.40 M','0.050 M','0.20 M'],ans:3,exp:'4.0 g ÷ 40 g/mol = 0.10 mol, and **M = mol ÷ L** = 0.10 mol ÷ 0.500 L = **0.20 M**. Forgetting to convert mL to L, or dividing by 1 L, gives the wrong answers.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q102'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Which concentration unit does not change when a solution is heated?',choices:['Molarity','Molality','Normality','Grams per liter'],ans:1,exp:'**Molality** is moles of solute per kilogram of solvent, and mass does not change with temperature. Molarity, normality and g/L all use solution volume, which expands on heating.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q102b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Acetic acid is titrated with NaOH. What is the pH at the equivalence point?',choices:['Exactly 7, because the acid is fully neutralized','Less than 7, because excess acid remains','Greater than 7, because acetate is a weak base','Less than 7, because Na⁺ is a weak acid'],ans:2,exp:'At equivalence the solution contains acetate, the **conjugate base** of a weak acid, which hydrolyzes to make OH⁻, so **pH > 7**. Strong acid/strong base gives pH 7, and weak base/strong acid gives pH < 7.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q103'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'A weak acid with pKₐ = 4.8 is titrated with NaOH. What is the pH at the half-equivalence point?',choices:['2.4','7.0','9.2','4.8'],ans:3,exp:'At half-equivalence, [HA] = [A⁻], so by the **Henderson–Hasselbalch equation** pH = pKₐ + log(1) = **pKₐ = 4.8**. This is also the point of maximum buffer capacity.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q103b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'What is the conjugate base of benzoic acid, C₆H₅COOH?',choices:['C₆H₅COO⁻','C₆H₅COOH₂⁺','C₆H₅O⁻','C₆H₅⁻'],ans:0,exp:'A conjugate base is the acid **minus one H⁺**: removing the acidic carboxyl proton gives benzoate, **C₆H₅COO⁻**. C₆H₅COOH₂⁺ is the conjugate acid, formed by adding a proton.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q104'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'What is the conjugate acid of ammonia, NH₃?',choices:['NH₂⁻','NH₄⁺','N₂H₄','NH₃⁺'],ans:1,exp:'Adding one H⁺ to NH₃ gives **NH₄⁺**, the ammonium ion. NH₂⁻ (amide) is the conjugate base of ammonia, formed by removing a proton.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q104b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Oxalic acid, H₂C₂O₄ (90 g/mol), is diprotic. What is its equivalent weight in an acid–base titration?',choices:['90 g/eq','180 g/eq','45 g/eq','30 g/eq'],ans:2,exp:'**Equivalent weight = molar mass ÷ number of acidic protons** = 90 ÷ 2 = 45 g/eq. Each mole of oxalic acid therefore neutralizes 2 mol NaOH.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q105'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'What is the normality of 0.50 M H₂SO₄ in a complete neutralization?',choices:['0.25 N','0.50 N','2.0 N','1.0 N'],ans:3,exp:'Normality = molarity × equivalents per mole; H₂SO₄ donates **2 H⁺**, so 0.50 M × 2 = **1.0 N**. Dividing instead of multiplying gives the 0.25 N trap.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q105b'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Which element is in the p-block of the periodic table?',choices:['Aluminum','Magnesium','Titanium','Calcium'],ans:0,exp:'**Aluminum** (group 13) has the valence configuration 3s² 3p¹, so its last electron enters a p orbital. Mg and Ca are s-block (groups 1–2) and Ti is a d-block transition metal.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q106'},
  {s:'Atoms, Periodic Trends & Bonding',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'What is the valence electron configuration of a neutral aluminum atom?',choices:['3s² 3p³','3s² 3p¹','3s¹ 3p²','3s² 3d¹'],ans:1,exp:'Al (Z = 13) is [Ne] **3s² 3p¹**, with three valence electrons, which is why it typically forms Al³⁺. The 3s orbital fills before 3p, and 3d is not occupied until after 4s.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q106b'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry & Redox',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'What is the oxidation number of aluminum in Na₃[AlF₆]?',choices:['+6','−3','+1','+3'],ans:3,exp:'Each Na is +1 and each F is −1: 3(+1) + x + 6(−1) = 0, so **x = +3**. Counting the six ligands as positive, or ignoring the counter-ions, gives the wrong value.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q107'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry & Redox',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'What is the oxidation number of manganese in KMnO₄?',choices:['+5','+7','+6','+4'],ans:1,exp:'K is +1 and each O is −2: +1 + x + 4(−2) = 0, so **x = +7**. Permanganate is a strong oxidizing agent precisely because Mn is in such a high oxidation state.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q107b'},
  {s:'Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'A central metal ion is bonded to six ligands with no lone pairs on the metal, as in [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻. What is its geometry?',choices:['Hexagonal planar','Trigonal bipyramidal','Octahedral','Tetrahedral'],ans:2,exp:'Six electron domains arrange themselves as far apart as possible in an **octahedral** geometry, with 90° bond angles. Trigonal bipyramidal fits five domains and tetrahedral fits four.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q108'},
  {s:'Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'XeF₄ has six electron domains around Xe, two of which are lone pairs. What is its molecular geometry?',choices:['Square planar','Tetrahedral','Seesaw','Octahedral'],ans:0,exp:'The electron geometry is octahedral, and the two lone pairs sit **opposite each other (trans)** to minimize repulsion, leaving the four F atoms in a **square planar** arrangement. A seesaw shape arises from five domains with one lone pair, as in SF₄.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q108b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'What is the mass percent of aluminum in Al₂O₃? (Al = 27, O = 16 g/mol)',choices:['26.5%','47.1%','64.0%','52.9%'],ans:3,exp:'Al₂O₃ has a molar mass of 2(27) + 3(16) = 102 g/mol, of which 54 g is Al: 54/102 = **52.9%**. So producing 100 kg of Al requires about 100/0.529 ≈ 189 kg of Al₂O₃; 47.1% is the oxygen fraction.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q109'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'What mass of Fe₂O₃ (160 g/mol) is needed to produce 84 g of iron? (Fe = 56 g/mol)',choices:['60 g','120 g','240 g','84 g'],ans:1,exp:'84 g Fe ÷ 56 g/mol = 1.5 mol Fe, and each Fe₂O₃ contains **2 Fe**, so 0.75 mol Fe₂O₃ × 160 g/mol = **120 g** is needed. Using a 1:1 Fe : Fe₂O₃ ratio would double the answer to 240 g.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q109b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'In the reaction Al(OH)₃ + OH⁻ → [Al(OH)₄]⁻, aluminum hydroxide acts as a:',choices:['Lewis base','Brønsted base','Lewis acid','reducing agent'],ans:2,exp:'Al accepts an electron pair from OH⁻ to form a new Al–O bond, so Al(OH)₃ is a **Lewis acid** (electron-pair acceptor). No oxidation numbers change, so it is not a redox reaction.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q110'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Which hydroxide dissolves in both excess strong acid and excess strong base?',choices:['Mg(OH)₂','Ni(OH)₂','Ca(OH)₂','Al(OH)₃'],ans:3,exp:'**Al(OH)₃ is amphoteric**: it reacts with H⁺ to give Al³⁺ and with OH⁻ to give [Al(OH)₄]⁻. Mg(OH)₂, Ca(OH)₂ and Ni(OH)₂ are basic hydroxides that dissolve in acid but not in excess base.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q110b'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry & Redox',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4C',q:'In an electrolytic cell used to plate a metal from its cations, at which electrode is the metal deposited, and what is its sign?',choices:['The cathode, which is negative','The anode, which is positive','The cathode, which is positive','The anode, which is negative'],ans:0,exp:'Metal deposition is **reduction**, so it always occurs at the **cathode**; in an electrolytic cell the cathode is connected to the negative terminal. In a galvanic cell the cathode is still where reduction happens, but it is the positive electrode.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q111'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry & Redox',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4C',q:'About how much charge is needed to deposit 1.0 mol of aluminum from Al³⁺? (F ≈ 96,500 C/mol e⁻)',choices:['9.7 × 10⁴ C','1.9 × 10⁵ C','2.9 × 10⁵ C','3.2 × 10⁴ C'],ans:2,exp:'Al³⁺ + **3e⁻** → Al, so 1.0 mol Al needs 3.0 mol electrons: 3 × 96,500 ≈ **2.9 × 10⁵ C**. Using one electron per ion gives the 9.7 × 10⁴ C trap.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q111b'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry & Redox',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4C',q:'An oxygen sensor measures the current from O₂ reduction at its cathode. If the sample also contains another gas that is reduced at the same electrode, the reported O₂ level will be:',choices:['falsely low','falsely high','unaffected','zero'],ans:1,exp:'The sensor cannot tell which species accepted the electrons, so the extra reduction adds current and the O₂ reading is **falsely high**. Such **interference** lowers the accuracy of the measurement.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q112'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry & Redox',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4C',q:'An interfering gas makes every reading of an O₂ sensor about 5% too high, while repeated readings agree closely with one another. The measurements are:',choices:['accurate but not precise','both accurate and precise','precise but not accurate','neither accurate nor precise'],ans:2,exp:'Close agreement between replicates means high **precision**, but a consistent offset from the true value is a **systematic error** that reduces **accuracy**. Random errors reduce precision; systematic errors reduce accuracy.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q112b'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry & Redox',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4C',q:'In a silver oxide battery, Ag₂O is reduced to Ag and zinc is oxidized to ZnO. Which electrode is the anode?',choices:['Silver oxide, because it is reduced','Zinc, because it is reduced','Silver oxide, because it is oxidized','Zinc, because it is oxidized'],ans:3,exp:'The **anode is where oxidation occurs**, so the zinc electrode is the anode and the silver oxide electrode, where reduction occurs, is the cathode. Identifying either half-reaction tells you both electrodes.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q113'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry & Redox',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4C',q:'A galvanic cell uses Ag⁺/Ag (E° = +0.80 V) and Cd²⁺/Cd (E° = −0.40 V). What is E°cell?',choices:['+1.20 V','+0.40 V','−1.20 V','+2.00 V'],ans:0,exp:'Ag⁺ has the higher reduction potential, so it is reduced at the cathode and Cd is oxidized at the anode: **E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode** = 0.80 − (−0.40) = **+1.20 V**. Reduction potentials are not multiplied by stoichiometric coefficients.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q113b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'A solution is 0.5% solute by mass. What is this concentration in parts per million (ppm)?',choices:['500 ppm','50,000 ppm','5,000 ppm','50 ppm'],ans:2,exp:'Percent is parts per hundred, so 0.5/100 = x/10⁶ gives **x = 5,000 ppm**; equivalently, **1% = 10,000 ppm**. Off-by-a-factor-of-ten errors are the usual trap.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q114'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'A dilute water sample contains 3 mg of lead per liter. What is the lead concentration in ppm?',choices:['3,000 ppm','0.003 ppm','300 ppm','3 ppm'],ans:3,exp:'One liter of dilute aqueous solution has a mass of about 1 kg = 10⁶ mg, so **1 mg/L ≈ 1 ppm** and 3 mg/L ≈ **3 ppm**. This shortcut holds only for dilute water solutions with density ≈ 1 g/mL.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q114b'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry & Redox',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'What is the oxidation state of carbon in methane, CH₄?',choices:['−4','+4','0','−2'],ans:0,exp:'Each H is +1, so x + 4(+1) = 0 and **x = −4**, the lowest possible oxidation state of carbon. Because carbon in CH₄ cannot gain more electrons, methane cannot be reduced further, only oxidized.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q115'},
  {s:'Electrochemistry & Redox',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'In which compound does carbon have the highest oxidation state?',choices:['CO','CH₃OH','HCHO','CO₂'],ans:3,exp:'In **CO₂**, carbon is **+4**, its maximum, because it has given up all four valence electrons to oxygen. Carbon is +2 in CO, 0 in formaldehyde and −2 in methanol; more C–O bonds means a more oxidized carbon.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q115b'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'How does adding a catalyst affect a reversible reaction?',choices:['It lowers Eₐ and increases K','It lowers ΔH and leaves K unchanged','It lowers Eₐ for both directions and leaves K unchanged','It raises the energy of the reactants'],ans:2,exp:'A catalyst provides an alternative pathway with a **lower activation energy for both forward and reverse reactions**, so both rates rise equally and **K is unchanged**. It does not change ΔH, ΔG or the energies of reactants and products.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q116'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'Which quantity does a catalyst change?',choices:['The time needed to reach equilibrium','The equilibrium yield of product','The ΔG of the reaction','The ΔH of the reaction'],ans:0,exp:'By speeding up both directions, a catalyst lets the system **reach equilibrium faster**, but the equilibrium composition is the same. ΔG, ΔH and the equilibrium yield are state properties that depend only on reactants and products.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q116b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'What is the conjugate base of the hydrogen sulfate ion, HSO₄⁻?',choices:['H₂SO₄','SO₄²⁻','HSO₃⁻','SO₃²⁻'],ans:1,exp:'Removing one H⁺ from HSO₄⁻ gives **SO₄²⁻**, with the charge becoming one unit more negative. H₂SO₄ is the conjugate acid, and the sulfite species have one fewer oxygen, so they are different compounds.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q117'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Which statement about the ionization of sulfuric acid in water is correct?',choices:['Both ionizations are essentially complete','Both ionizations are only partial','The first ionization is partial and the second is complete','The first ionization is complete and the second is partial'],ans:3,exp:'H₂SO₄ is a **strong acid** for its first proton, but HSO₄⁻ is a **weak acid** (Kₐ ≈ 1 × 10⁻²) because removing H⁺ from an anion is harder. For polyprotic acids, each successive Kₐ is smaller.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q117b'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'Water vapor at −1 °C is compressed at constant temperature to very high pressure. In what order does it change phase?',choices:['Vapor → liquid → solid','Vapor → solid → liquid','Vapor → liquid only','Vapor → solid only'],ans:1,exp:'At −1 °C, slightly below the triple point temperature, vapor first deposits as ice; because water\'s **solid–liquid line has a negative slope**, further compression melts the ice. Ice is less dense than liquid water, so high pressure favors the liquid.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q118'},
  {s:'Thermodynamics, Kinetics & Equilibrium',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'Why does solid CO₂ (dry ice) sublime rather than melt at 1 atm?',choices:['Its triple-point pressure is above 1 atm','Its critical pressure is below 1 atm','Its solid–liquid line has a negative slope','Its triple-point temperature is above 25 °C'],ans:0,exp:'CO₂\'s **triple point is at about 5.1 atm**, so at 1 atm the liquid phase is not stable at any temperature, and the solid passes directly to gas. Unlike water, CO₂ has a positively sloped solid–liquid line.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q118b'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'What is the pH of 0.010 M KOH at 25 °C?',choices:['2','10','12','11'],ans:2,exp:'KOH is a strong base, so [OH⁻] = 0.010 M and **pOH = 2**; then pH = 14 − 2 = **12**. Stopping at pOH gives the tempting value of 2.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q119'},
  {s:'Acids, Bases & Solubility',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'What is the pH of 0.050 M Ba(OH)₂ at 25 °C?',choices:['12.7','13','1','11'],ans:1,exp:'Each Ba(OH)₂ releases **two OH⁻**, so [OH⁻] = 0.10 M, pOH = 1 and **pH = 13**. Using 0.050 M OH⁻ gives the incorrect 12.7.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q119b'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4B',q:'Which is an assumption of the kinetic molecular theory of ideal gases?',choices:['Gas particles attract each other at short range','Gas particles lose kinetic energy in each collision','Gas particles have negligible volume and no attractions','Gas particles occupy most of the container volume'],ans:2,exp:'An ideal gas is modeled as point particles with **negligible volume** and **no intermolecular forces** whose collisions are **perfectly elastic**. The molar volume of 22.4 L/mol applies only at STP (0 °C, 1 atm), not at 25 °C.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q120'},
  {s:'Stoichiometry, Solutions & Gases',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4B',q:'Under which conditions does a real gas deviate most from ideal behavior?',choices:['High pressure and low temperature','Low pressure and high temperature','Low pressure and low temperature','High pressure and high temperature'],ans:0,exp:'At **high pressure** the particles\' own volume becomes significant, and at **low temperature** attractions matter because the particles move slowly. Gases behave most ideally at low pressure and high temperature.',src:'aamc-chem-qpack:Q120b'},
  // AAMC Test Concepts: Chemistry. Added 2026-11-11 after auditing the deck against the chemistry actually
  // tested on the AAMC Sample Test (Chem/Phys) and Independent Question Bank. Scenario-style "which rule applies?"
  // cards for concepts the deck was missing or thin on. Original wording; no AAMC text.
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'An electron is excited from n = 2 to n = 4. What does the change in n mainly tell you?',choices:['It now spends more time farther from the nucleus','Its orbital changed shape from a sphere to a dumbbell','Its spin reversed direction during the jump upward','The nucleus it orbits gained two additional protons'],ans:0,exp:'The principal quantum number **n** sets the shell: its **size and energy**. Bigger n = farther out. Shape is set by **l**, spin by **mₛ**, and n says nothing about the nucleus.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:ST29'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Two electrons have the same n but different l values. What differs between their orbitals?',choices:['Their average distance from the nucleus','Their shape (s, p, d or f)','The electric charge of each electron','The number of neutrons nearby'],ans:1,exp:'**l** (angular momentum) sets the orbital **shape**: l = 0 is s, 1 is p, 2 is d. The same n means roughly the same size. Every electron has the same charge, and neutrons are irrelevant.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:ST29b'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4C',q:'In an electroplating cell, 0.6 mol of electrons flows. How many moles of Cu²⁺ become copper metal?',choices:['0.6 mol','1.2 mol','0.3 mol','0.2 mol'],ans:2,exp:'One **faraday** = one mole of electrons. Each Cu²⁺ needs **2** electrons, so moles of Cu = 0.6 ÷ 2 = **0.3 mol**. Multiplying by 2 (1.2) is the common trap; 0.2 would fit a 3+ ion.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB5'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4C',q:'A factory wants to plate silver onto spoons twice as fast. Which change does that most directly?',choices:['Using a larger anode made of silver','Adding more nitrate ions to the bath','Using a larger spoon as the cathode','Doubling the current through the cell'],ans:3,exp:'Plating rate = electrons delivered per second = **current**. A larger electrode gives metal more room to land but supplies no extra electrons, and spectator ions like nitrate are not plated.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB22'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4B',q:'Two gases leak through the same pinhole. Gas P has a lower density than gas Q at the same conditions. What happens?',choices:['P escapes faster because its molecules are lighter','Q escapes faster because its molecules are heavier','Both escape at the same rate at one temperature','P escapes slower because it has fewer molecules'],ans:0,exp:'At the same temperature all gases have the same average kinetic energy, so **lighter molecules move faster** (Graham\'s law: rate ∝ 1/√M). Lower density at the same conditions means lower molar mass.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB19'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Which salt would dissolve noticeably better in dilute acid than in pure water?',choices:['Silver chloride, AgCl, a halide salt','Magnesium hydroxide, Mg(OH)₂','Sodium nitrate, NaNO₃, very soluble','Potassium bromide, KBr, a halide salt'],ans:1,exp:'Acid grabs **basic anions** (OH⁻, CO₃²⁻, F⁻) and pulls the dissolving reaction forward (Le Châtelier). OH⁻ is very basic. Cl⁻, Br⁻ and NO₃⁻ come from strong acids, so added acid doesn\'t help them dissolve.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB21'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Solid PbCl₂ sits at equilibrium with its saturated solution. Some NaCl is stirred in. What happens to the amount of dissolved lead?',choices:['It rises, as sodium ions attract chloride','It stays the same, because Ksp is constant','It falls, as extra Cl⁻ pushes PbCl₂ back to solid','It rises, because more total salt is now dissolved'],ans:2,exp:'This is the **common ion effect**: extra Cl⁻ raises the ion product above Ksp, so PbCl₂ precipitates until it is back at Ksp, leaving **less** Pb²⁺ dissolved. Ksp stays constant; the amount dissolved does not.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB21b'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'Two solutions are mixed. The product [Ba²⁺][SO₄²⁻] in the mixture is larger than Ksp for BaSO₄. What will be observed?',choices:['Nothing happens; both ions stay in solution','Ksp rises until it matches the mixture','Barium is oxidized by the sulfate ion','A solid forms until the product equals Ksp'],ans:3,exp:'If the **ion product > Ksp**, the solution is supersaturated and solid **precipitates** until the product equals Ksp. Ksp only changes with temperature, and no redox happens here.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:ST3'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'Rate = k[A]²[B]⁰. Doubling [B] while holding [A] fixed changes the rate by what factor?',choices:['No change, since B\'s exponent is zero','It doubles, because B doubled','It quadruples, because of the 2','It halves, as B is used up faster'],ans:0,exp:'Each exponent says how the rate responds to that reactant. **[B]⁰ = 1**, so B has no effect. Doubling A would quadruple the rate (2²). Overall order = 2 + 0 = **2**.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB1'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'A rate law is Rate = k[X][Y]². What is the overall reaction order?',choices:['Second order','Third order','First order','It depends on k'],ans:1,exp:'**Overall order = add the exponents**: 1 + 2 = 3. The rate constant k never sets the order; it changes only with temperature or a catalyst.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB1b'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Which valence configuration shows an atom in an excited state?',choices:['3s² 3p¹','4s² 3d¹','3s² 3d¹','2s² 2p⁴'],ans:2,exp:'After 3s comes **3p**, not 3d. 3s² 3d¹ skipped 3p, so an electron was boosted up = **excited**. 4s → 3d is the normal filling order, and s → p with the same number is normal.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB13'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Which configuration is the ground state of a 7-electron atom?',choices:['1s² 2s¹ 2p⁴','1s¹ 2s² 2p⁴','1s² 2s² 2p² 3s¹','1s² 2s² 2p³'],ans:3,exp:'Fill from the bottom with no gaps: 1s → 2s → 2p. A half-empty lower orbital (1s¹ or 2s¹) or an electron in 3s before 2p is full describes an **excited** state.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB3'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'A carbon has four single bonds and no lone pairs. What are its geometry and hybridization?',choices:['Tetrahedral, sp³','Trigonal planar, sp²','Linear, sp','Octahedral, sp³d²'],ans:0,exp:'Count the **electron domains**: 4 → sp³ → **tetrahedral** (109.5°). 3 → sp², trigonal planar; 2 → sp, linear. Octahedral needs 6.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB12'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'An alkene carbon is double-bonded to one carbon and single-bonded to two hydrogens. How many electron domains does it have, and what is its shape?',choices:['Four, tetrahedral','Three, trigonal planar','Two, linear','Five, trigonal bipyramidal'],ans:1,exp:'A double bond counts as **one** electron domain. 1 double + 2 single = **3** domains → **sp²**, trigonal planar (120°).',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB12b'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'A sugar has 4 chiral centers and no internal symmetry. What is the maximum number of stereoisomers?',choices:['4','8','16','32'],ans:2,exp:'Maximum stereoisomers = **2ⁿ**, where n = chiral centers: 2⁴ = **16**. 8 and 32 are 2³ and 2⁵, the miscount traps.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:ST2'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'Which disaccharide gives NO silver mirror with Tollens\' reagent?',choices:['Maltose','Lactose','Cellobiose','Sucrose'],ans:3,exp:'Tollens\' reagent (Ag⁺ in NH₃) detects **reducing sugars**, which have a free anomeric carbon that can open to an aldehyde. In **sucrose**, glucose and fructose are joined through **both** anomeric carbons, so neither can open. Maltose, lactose and cellobiose each keep one free.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:ST16'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'A 30-carbon molecule built from isoprene units folds into four fused rings. What is the product most likely to be?',choices:['Cholesterol, a steroid','A triacylglycerol (fat)','A membrane phospholipid','A prostaglandin signal'],ans:0,exp:'**Isoprene (terpene) → squalene (C₃₀) → cholesterol → steroid hormones.** Fats, phospholipids and prostaglandins are built from fatty acids, not isoprene.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:ST14'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4B',q:'A sealed, rigid steel tank of gas is heated. What happens?',choices:['Volume rises; pressure stays the same','Pressure rises; volume stays the same','Both volume and pressure stay the same','Volume falls as the molecules speed up'],ans:1,exp:'In a **rigid** container the volume can\'t change, so heating raises the **pressure** (P ∝ T). Volume rises with temperature only when pressure is held constant. Always check which variable is fixed.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB9'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4B',q:'At very high pressure, a real gas occupies a slightly larger volume than PV = nRT predicts. Why?',choices:['The molecules repel the container walls','The molecules attract one another strongly','The molecules themselves take up space','The temperature rises during compression'],ans:2,exp:'The ideal gas law assumes **zero molecular volume**. Squeezed hard, the molecules\' own size matters, so the real volume is larger. Attractions do the **opposite** (they make the volume smaller), mostly at low temperature.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB8'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4B',q:'Gas is collected in an upside-down tube over water. The water level inside the tube is higher than outside. Compared with the air pressure, the gas pressure is:',choices:['higher, by the weight of the water column','the same, because the tube is open below','higher, because the gas is warm and expands','lower, by the extra column of water'],ans:3,exp:'Outside air pressure = gas pressure + ρgh of the extra water. Water standing **higher** inside means the gas pushes **less** than the air. Like a straw: low pressure above, liquid rises.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:ST23'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5B',q:'CH₃OH boils at 65 °C, while CH₃SH boils at 6 °C. What best explains the difference?',choices:['Hydrogen bonds hold CH₃OH molecules together','The C–O bond is stronger than the C–S bond in each molecule','CH₃SH has the higher molar mass of the two','Sulfur atoms are larger than oxygen atoms'],ans:0,exp:'Boiling separates molecules; it doesn\'t break bonds **inside** them. O–H can **hydrogen bond** (H on F, O or N); S–H can\'t. If mass decided it, the heavier CH₃SH would boil higher.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB7'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Which atom releases the most energy when it gains one electron?',choices:['Nitrogen','Chlorine','Sodium','Neon'],ans:1,exp:'Electron affinity is highest near the **top right**, just short of the noble gases. **Chlorine** needs one electron to fill its shell. Neon is already full, sodium would rather lose an electron, and nitrogen\'s half-filled p shell resists an extra one.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB16'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Moving from sodium to argon across period 3, what happens to atomic radius and first ionization energy overall?',choices:['Radius grows; ionization energy falls sharply','Both grow','Radius shrinks; ionization energy rises','Both shrink'],ans:2,exp:'Across a row: more protons, same shell → stronger pull (higher effective nuclear charge) → a **smaller** atom that holds its electrons **harder**. Down a column is the reverse.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB14'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'Which ion has the same number of electrons as argon (18)?',choices:['Na⁺','Mg²⁺','F⁻','K⁺'],ans:3,exp:'Electrons = atomic number − charge. K is 19, so **K⁺ has 18**. Na⁺, Mg²⁺ and F⁻ each have 10, like neon.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB18'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5A',q:'An indicator HIn has pKa = 9.1. Around what pH does it change color?',choices:['About 9','About 7','About 4.9','About 14'],ans:0,exp:'An indicator is half HIn and half In⁻ (its color midpoint) when **pH = its pKa**. Choose an indicator whose pKa is near the equivalence-point pH of the titration.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB4'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'200 g of water (c = 4.2 J/g·°C) warms from 20 °C to 30 °C. How much heat did it absorb?',choices:['840 J','8,400 J','25,200 J','84 J'],ans:1,exp:'**q = mcΔT** = 200 × 4.2 × 10 = **8,400 J**. Use the temperature **change** (10 °C), not 30 °C, which would give 25,200 J.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:ST36'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'A mutant enzyme has the same Km as the normal enzyme but one-third the Vmax. What changed?',choices:['It binds substrate much more weakly','It binds substrate much more tightly','It turns bound substrate over more slowly','It needs a higher substrate level to bind'],ans:2,exp:'**Km = grip** (the substrate level at half Vmax; same Km = same binding). **Vmax = speed** when saturated. Only Vmax dropped, so catalysis slowed, not binding.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:ST19'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'5E',q:'On a Lineweaver–Burk plot, three enzyme variants share an x-intercept but have different y-intercepts. What differs among them?',choices:['Km only','Both Km and Vmax','Neither one','Vmax only'],ans:3,exp:'y-intercept = **1/Vmax**; x-intercept = **−1/Km**. Same x, different y → same Km, different **Vmax**. Hook: **V**ertical = **V**max.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:ST20'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'A hemiacetal reacts with methanol whose oxygen is ¹⁸O-labeled, forming an acetal and water. Where does the ¹⁸O end up?',choices:['In the OCH₃ group of the acetal','In the water molecule released','In the ring oxygen of the acetal','Split evenly between water and ring'],ans:0,exp:'Follow the label\'s **neighbor**: the ¹⁸O stays bonded to its CH₃, so it ends up in the new **OCH₃** group. The OH that leaves as water came from the hemiacetal.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB23'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'gen_chem',aamc:'4E',q:'A compound has the molecular formula C₆H₁₂O₆. What is its empirical formula?',choices:['CHO','CH₂O','C₃H₆O₃','C₂H₄O₂'],ans:1,exp:'Divide every subscript by the largest common factor (6): **CH₂O**. Turning everything into 1s (CHO) changes the ratio, and C₃H₆O₃ or C₂H₄O₂ can still be divided further.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB15'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'A linear peptide is made of 5 amino acids. How many peptide bonds does it contain?',choices:['5','6','4','3'],ans:2,exp:'**Peptide bonds = amino acids − 1** (5 beads on a string need 4 links). Forming each bond also releases one water.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB24'},
  {s:'AAMC Test Concepts',disc:'organic_chem',aamc:'5D',q:'In CH₃CH₂C(O)CH₃, the C=O carbon is bonded to two other carbons. Which functional group is present?',choices:['Aldehyde','Carboxylic acid','Ester','Ketone'],ans:3,exp:'C=O **in the middle** (carbons on both sides) = **ketone**. At the end of a chain (with an H) = aldehyde; with OH on the same carbon = carboxylic acid; with O–C on it = ester.',src:'aamc-test-concepts:IQB25'},
];
